WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:50.599
[rock music]

00:00:52.824 --> 00:00:56.223
[Peter] Bill, you're fourth generation.
You know it inside and out.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:57.673
So we're gonna see it through your eyes.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.017
[Bill] Memphis is a great place.

00:01:00.084 --> 00:01:03.186
This is Main Street, it was once traffic

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.940
and then it got closed off

00:01:06.007 --> 00:01:08.401
and made for pedestrian work only.

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:10.663
-So you have some stuff falling apart.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.179
Businesses have moved out.

00:01:12.246 --> 00:01:13.996
What was this before down here?

00:01:14.063 --> 00:01:17.664
I mean, at one time when cotton was king,

00:01:17.731 --> 00:01:19.882
this was the home of

00:01:19.949 --> 00:01:23.696
massive amounts of business
and trading and organization.

00:01:24.155 --> 00:01:27.596
After Martin Luther King's
assassination in '68,

00:01:28.376 --> 00:01:30.824
Downtown Memphis became a ghost town.

00:01:30.891 --> 00:01:33.891
It wasn't until like the late 80s

00:01:34.411 --> 00:01:37.191
that reinvestment started back in downtown.

00:01:37.264 --> 00:01:39.128
And the problem is

00:01:39.594 --> 00:01:42.736
that 25 year dead period

00:01:42.803 --> 00:01:45.808
created a lot of what
people think Memphis is,

00:01:45.875 --> 00:01:49.899
which is not a crime ridden, nasty place.

00:01:50.286 --> 00:01:54.093
-But on paper, it is one of the worst
homicide rates in the country.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:55.215
It's gotten better.

00:01:55.282 --> 00:01:57.196
-Yeah.
-Like most big cities right now.

00:01:57.263 --> 00:01:59.386
But technically it is, right?

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.500
The statistics will tell you

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:06.453
annually it's one of the top five most
dangerous places in terms of violent crime.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.426
You can't run from that fact.

00:02:08.493 --> 00:02:11.743
That does not mean that people are
walking around getting mugged.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.703
Like most crime,
it's people that know people.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:15.951
The problem is,

00:02:17.464 --> 00:02:21.177
as the city went through
its problems in the 70s and 80s,

00:02:21.244 --> 00:02:22.541
poverty grew.

00:02:22.608 --> 00:02:24.432
-Lack of access grew.
-Yep.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.506
And because of the growth of those things,

00:02:28.966 --> 00:02:30.133
crime followed it.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.159
And the city hadn't gotten over it yet.

00:02:32.226 --> 00:02:34.421
But it's working hard to do that.

00:02:34.488 --> 00:02:37.368
People don't know the rich cultural,

00:02:37.435 --> 00:02:40.393
musical business heritage.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:44.253
This place was boarded up until 1984.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.909
And it's where a local family

00:02:46.976 --> 00:02:49.941
decided Downtown, Memphis
was reinvesting in.

00:02:50.008 --> 00:02:52.890
And it's a South's Grand Hotel
called the Peabody,

00:02:53.403 --> 00:02:55.459
started in the early 1900s

00:02:56.078 --> 00:02:59.242
and shut down when downtown shut down.

00:02:59.616 --> 00:03:03.333
And this became the anchor
for Memphis Revitalization

00:03:03.848 --> 00:03:07.086
and it is one of
the finest hotels in the world.

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:08.186
-This is beautiful.

00:03:08.253 --> 00:03:13.527
-And something you might want to know
is in the center of this place

00:03:13.594 --> 00:03:15.870
is a fountain and there's ducks in it.

00:03:15.937 --> 00:03:16.820
-Seriously?

00:03:16.887 --> 00:03:20.962
There's reason there's ducks in it…
and they're trained to go upstairs

00:03:21.029 --> 00:03:23.244
to the Duck Palace
and come down twice a day.

00:03:23.311 --> 00:03:24.873
-The Duck Palace?
-Yeah.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:25.926
It's on the roof.

00:03:25.993 --> 00:03:27.446
-They live up there?
-They do.

00:03:27.513 --> 00:03:30.752
And the reason it happened
is back in the 30s

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:34.155
some hunters brought some ducks back,
threw them in this thing…

00:03:34.222 --> 00:03:35.570
-Oh yeah?
-Oh look.

00:03:35.637 --> 00:03:38.618
-They're about to come down.
-[woman] Is this for the ducks?

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:39.573
I think so. Yeah.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.407
Yeah, the ducks are coming down soon

00:03:41.474 --> 00:03:46.418
and so all these people are here every day
to watch the march of the ducks.

00:03:47.199 --> 00:03:48.603
-Mostly tourists, right?

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:50.515
I don't know where you from?

00:03:50.582 --> 00:03:52.020
-We're from Wisconsin.
-Yes.

00:03:52.997 --> 00:03:56.703
For Wisconsin
but there's some locals here for sure.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.126
There's the Duck Commander?

00:03:58.193 --> 00:04:00.406
-That's him!
-Hey, Duck Commander, say hello.

00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:02.971
I'm Bill, fourth generation Memphian.

00:04:03.038 --> 00:04:04.806
-Good to meet you.
-Good to see you.

00:04:04.873 --> 00:04:06.566
-Hey, I'm Peter.

00:04:06.633 --> 00:04:08.313
Peter has a show that reaches…

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:10.541
-How long you been doing this?
-Seven years.

00:04:10.608 --> 00:04:13.280
-How's it going?
-Man, every day I wake up, it's a go.

00:04:13.347 --> 00:04:15.704
Do you have
a good relationship with the Ducks?

00:04:15.771 --> 00:04:17.571
I do. I get new Ducks every 90 days.

00:04:17.638 --> 00:04:21.856
So it's my job to break them from being
a scared team that's unsure of themselves,

00:04:21.923 --> 00:04:25.653
to where they're marching with such pride
that it's worthy of a red carpet.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.527
And it's happening right now?
It's about to happen now.

00:04:28.594 --> 00:04:29.482
Don't go nowhere.

00:04:29.549 --> 00:04:32.544
I'm glad we bumped into you.
Completely by accident.

00:04:32.611 --> 00:04:35.475
-I appreciate what you do for our city.
-I appreciate you.

00:04:35.836 --> 00:04:38.758
So what happens is, Kenan,
who's the Duck Master,

00:04:39.237 --> 00:04:41.524
will march the ducks down from the roof.

00:04:41.956 --> 00:04:45.321
They will come down the elevator,
walk across the red carpet,

00:04:45.388 --> 00:04:49.719
to much fanfare, swim around in the
fountain until the end of the afternoon,

00:04:49.786 --> 00:04:51.424
and then they'll go home.

00:04:51.491 --> 00:04:52.726
Oh, that's it, right?

00:04:53.324 --> 00:04:55.016
[Bill laughs]

00:04:55.083 --> 00:04:57.436
[Peter] The Duck Elevator. This is wild.

00:04:58.231 --> 00:05:01.937
[Kenan] All right, duckies,
let's put some smiles on some faces.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:10.476
Everybody say good morning, duckies!

00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:12.367
[crowd] Good morning, duckies.

00:05:12.882 --> 00:05:13.765
[applause]

00:05:16.167 --> 00:05:19.222
And you know how Hollywood
has its walk of fame?

00:05:19.289 --> 00:05:20.317
With the stars?

00:05:20.384 --> 00:05:23.370
Memphis has its walk of fame,
and it's duck feet,

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.699
and its people who've mattered in Memphis.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.551
All right, guys, the time has finally come
where we get to meet up in person.

00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:38.913
The Your Fellow Americans book tour
kicking off early August.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:43.751
New York, Boston, greater Detroit,
greater Chicago, greater Phoenix.

00:05:44.299 --> 00:05:46.183
More locations coming.

00:05:46.796 --> 00:05:49.860
You can buy your ticket here
with the QR code with your phone

00:05:49.927 --> 00:05:52.229
or link down below in the description.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:55.345
Here's what you get with the ticket.
You get a book.

00:05:55.412 --> 00:05:57.407
You get a signed copy from me.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.952
Your choice, handshake or high five.

00:06:01.841 --> 00:06:04.663
And just a good time hanging out together

00:06:05.046 --> 00:06:06.533
in the flesh and blood.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.600
The biggest problem with video making is

00:06:08.667 --> 00:06:11.180
I'm on this side of the screen
you're on that side,

00:06:11.247 --> 00:06:13.783
and there's no interaction,
well, this is gonna change that.

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:17.440
It's gonna be a great celebration
for our country's birthday

00:06:17.507 --> 00:06:20.683
with positive stories about the US

00:06:21.083 --> 00:06:23.733
If you're not seeing your location,
I'm working on it.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.351
It's the publisher
that comes up with the locations.

00:06:26.418 --> 00:06:29.950
Really, that comes down to
the more tickets bought,

00:06:30.017 --> 00:06:32.833
the more locations they're gonna add.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.052
So, hey, if you want to help out
your fellow Americans,

00:06:36.119 --> 00:06:39.424
those in the cities I mentioned,
and you're thinking of going,

00:06:39.492 --> 00:06:40.963
you know, bring a friend,

00:06:41.492 --> 00:06:43.213
bring a partner, bring whomever.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:45.522
Purchase here or down here.

00:06:45.589 --> 00:06:48.000
So looking forward to
meeting you in person.

00:06:48.067 --> 00:06:49.895
Now back to Memphis.

00:06:51.339 --> 00:06:53.456
[Bill] Memphians love to eat.

00:06:53.523 --> 00:06:55.084
Best barbecue in the world.

00:06:55.151 --> 00:06:59.008
Sorry, Texas. Sorry, North Carolina.
Sorry, the rest of you.

00:06:59.075 --> 00:07:01.493
-It's the best.
-Okay. We'll try some later.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:03.223
I'll judge it for you.

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:05.283
-We like to work. We like to hunt.

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:08.261
We like college football.
And we like to be entertained.

00:07:08.328 --> 00:07:11.538
And one of the greatest places
to be entertained is Beale Street,

00:07:11.605 --> 00:07:14.551
which is the home of
where rock and roll started.

00:07:15.011 --> 00:07:18.714
Tina Turner, Elvis Presley,
Jerry Lee Lewis, B.B. King.

00:07:19.192 --> 00:07:23.259
Just a couple of hours south is
the Crossroads, is the Mississippi Delta,

00:07:23.326 --> 00:07:24.966
where the blues started.

00:07:25.033 --> 00:07:26.219
And east of here

00:07:26.619 --> 00:07:30.744
is where the old banjo
twangy rockabilly started.

00:07:31.184 --> 00:07:35.747
And a very, very, very
heavy gospel influence.

00:07:35.814 --> 00:07:37.924
-Right.
-And they collided in Memphis.

00:07:38.439 --> 00:07:42.600
I never thought of the regional fusion
that made rock and roll.

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:43.925
Yeah. Nobody understands.

00:07:43.992 --> 00:07:47.507
You got the blues from the Delta,
hillbillies coming in, gospel.

00:07:47.574 --> 00:07:51.723
The church.
The deep, rich church gospel music.

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:55.486
And those three confluences
came together and created rock and roll.

00:07:55.553 --> 00:07:56.976
[siren blares]

00:08:00.447 --> 00:08:03.917
Main Street,
where we started, picks up here.

00:08:03.984 --> 00:08:07.652
And it's a complete revitalization

00:08:07.719 --> 00:08:11.616
of kind of a cool
entertainment arts district.

00:08:11.683 --> 00:08:14.280
-I had no idea
Memphis would be so interesting.

00:08:14.347 --> 00:08:16.270
Nobody does. You gotta get here.

00:08:16.337 --> 00:08:19.017
This is a place
you're gonna rip by on I-40.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:21.570
-Not worth stopping.
-It's too bad.

00:08:21.637 --> 00:08:23.116
I'm not thinking of Elvis.

00:08:23.183 --> 00:08:25.216
-I'm not a Graceland guy.
-All right.

00:08:25.283 --> 00:08:27.569
Along the lines of that history

00:08:27.636 --> 00:08:29.292
is our scar.

00:08:29.806 --> 00:08:31.469
Our deep, deep, deep scar.

00:08:31.536 --> 00:08:34.873
And certainly there's scars.
There's slavery, all kinds of scars.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:40.384
But the greatest scar
is on April 4th, 1968, at 6:01 PM

00:08:40.964 --> 00:08:43.630
Martin Luther King
was assassinated in our town.

00:08:44.138 --> 00:08:46.374
That happened at the Lorraine Motel,

00:08:46.441 --> 00:08:50.793
which has now been turned into
the National Civil Rights Museum.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:52.569
-Oh, that's where it happened.

00:08:52.636 --> 00:08:53.786
Right there.

00:08:53.853 --> 00:08:58.090
-That's not only where it happened,
but where that wreath is is the balcony.

00:08:58.157 --> 00:09:00.840
If you remember the iconic picture

00:09:00.907 --> 00:09:04.254
of Jesse Jackson
shooting toward the shooter…

00:09:04.321 --> 00:09:07.513
-…with Martin Luther King on the ground.
-Yep.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:09.142
I'm gonna introduce you

00:09:09.804 --> 00:09:14.330
to Jackie Smith, who was
the last resident of the Lorraine Motel

00:09:14.863 --> 00:09:18.414
when this thing shut down
and turned into the museum,

00:09:18.973 --> 00:09:22.233
who has been right here on the sidewalk

00:09:22.818 --> 00:09:26.324
ever since 1987 and has not moved.

00:09:26.747 --> 00:09:28.158
-Every day?
-Every day.

00:09:28.225 --> 00:09:29.545
Where does she sleep?

00:09:29.612 --> 00:09:30.495
Right there.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:33.646
-Huh.
-Come on. Let me introduce you to Jackie.

00:09:35.473 --> 00:09:36.442
Jackie!

00:09:36.509 --> 00:09:38.459
-What you doing?
-Doing good. Thank you.

00:09:38.526 --> 00:09:40.993
So good to see you. Meet my friend, Peter.

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:43.316
-Hey, Peter. 
-Nice to meet you, Jackie.

00:09:43.383 --> 00:09:46.265
-Oh, let me get my book.
-Okay. You get your book.

00:09:46.332 --> 00:09:47.215
[Bill laughs]

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:50.499
Jackie's story is this…

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:55.225
She lived when the hotel
was not the museum, but just the hotel.

00:09:55.292 --> 00:09:57.142
She was the last person to live here.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:01.254
She locked herself in for 30 days
and wouldn't come out.

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:04.618
Because she believed
that the millions of dollars

00:10:04.685 --> 00:10:07.246
that the city, county,
state, and federal government

00:10:07.313 --> 00:10:10.689
were gonna spend
converting Lorraine  Motel into a museum

00:10:11.113 --> 00:10:13.806
would better perpetuate
the memory of Dr. King

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:18.247
by making it a place
to help the disadvantaged.

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:21.340
And she locked herself in there
with cans of beans

00:10:21.407 --> 00:10:23.506
and wouldn't come out for 30 days.

00:10:23.573 --> 00:10:27.293
They finally removed her,
and they put all her stuff on the sidewalk.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.349
She wants the story of Dr. King

00:10:30.416 --> 00:10:34.026
not to be flashy
civil rights museum to the kids,

00:10:34.932 --> 00:10:35.907
but action.

00:10:35.974 --> 00:10:38.043
And so she is here today

00:10:38.476 --> 00:10:42.118
and has been for the last 35, 40 years

00:10:42.632 --> 00:10:46.774
to say, Dr. King wants action,

00:10:47.226 --> 00:10:48.307
not monuments.

00:10:48.374 --> 00:10:50.216
And that's what Jackie stands for.

00:10:50.283 --> 00:10:52.466
-Jackie, you got Bill's book.

00:10:52.533 --> 00:10:54.673
Oh, that's my book. She's in my book.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:56.566
-Oh, you're in the book? Okay, cool.

00:10:56.633 --> 00:10:59.101
He came out here early
when I started the protest.

00:10:59.168 --> 00:11:00.947
He spent the night out here as well.

00:11:01.014 --> 00:11:01.960
-Back in '86?

00:11:02.027 --> 00:11:04.492
-Back in '86,
you couldn't walk down these streets.

00:11:04.559 --> 00:11:06.563
Remember what Memphis was then, right?

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.491
-See that building down there?
-Yep.

00:11:09.558 --> 00:11:10.676
Past the White Van.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:14.713
There was hookers and everything
walking up and down through here.

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:17.770
And they put her stuff
out on the curb out there.

00:11:17.837 --> 00:11:21.642
And I saw it on the news,
and I thought, why is this Black woman

00:11:21.709 --> 00:11:25.063
protesting the Civil Rights Museum?
That doesn't make sense.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:26.803
So I came down here to ask her.

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:31.040
And her story was so compelling, I spent
the night here with her a couple of nights.

00:11:31.107 --> 00:11:32.520
Wrote a story about it,

00:11:32.587 --> 00:11:35.354
and it ended up getting published
in Life or something.

00:11:35.421 --> 00:11:36.662
I can't remember.
-Oh, cool.

00:11:36.729 --> 00:11:40.861
I'm basically here in protest
against the exploitation of Dr. King

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:44.217
and the gentrification of this area.

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:48.612
You see, they've turned this area
into a very expensive community

00:11:48.679 --> 00:11:50.489
where there's no affordable housing.

00:11:50.556 --> 00:11:52.879
There's no affordable housing
for us to be in.

00:11:52.946 --> 00:11:54.871
For the first 12 years,

00:11:54.938 --> 00:11:58.879
I was able to spend the night here
after they evicted me from my room.

00:11:59.227 --> 00:12:01.729
From the Lorraine Motel.
I was staying in room 301.

00:12:01.796 --> 00:12:07.243
And so, in order for me to be here,
I had to have accommodations to be here.

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:11.093
So when they put me out I went
down the street and got one of those rooms.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:13.440
And I could go back and forth
to the washroom,

00:12:13.507 --> 00:12:17.140
take a break, clean up,
and that sort of thing up until 2000.

00:12:17.528 --> 00:12:20.408
From 1988 to the year 2000,
I could do that.

00:12:20.475 --> 00:12:24.018
And then, all of a sudden,
the landlord came and told me,

00:12:24.085 --> 00:12:26.078
I've got to let that area go.

00:12:26.145 --> 00:12:28.207
I have no such accommodations now.

00:12:28.274 --> 00:12:32.560
And so now, that area has become
condominium, you see.

00:12:32.627 --> 00:12:34.950
And so there's nothing affordable
in this area.

00:12:35.017 --> 00:12:37.241
How  are you gonna have
a civil rights museum

00:12:37.308 --> 00:12:39.271
surrounded by gentrification, you see?

00:12:39.338 --> 00:12:41.603
-Did you know Dr. King?
-No, I did not.

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:44.177
I read Dr. King's books early on,

00:12:44.244 --> 00:12:47.861
so I just educated myself
on his teachings and principles.

00:12:48.268 --> 00:12:51.598
There have been people that have come here
from all walks of life,

00:12:51.665 --> 00:12:53.313
school children,

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:55.615
various religions, all over.

00:12:56.075 --> 00:12:58.525
So good to see you.
Buy yourself something to eat.

00:12:58.592 --> 00:13:02.413
-Thank you. All the best.
-Jackie, take care. All the best.

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:04.788
He's one of the few
that's carried on the work.

00:13:04.855 --> 00:13:07.563
-You love Bill?
-Absolutely. He's doing a great job.

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:08.806
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

00:13:11.263 --> 00:13:13.563
[Bill] I found two friends
that I drug with us.

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:16.352
-[woman] I just missed them.
-I knew you wouldn't mind.

00:13:16.419 --> 00:13:19.569
-Here we go, guys. The Orpheum.
-The guy on the street is Peter.

00:13:19.636 --> 00:13:23.089
-I assume so. Kristen. Nice to meet you.
-So good to see you.

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:26.741
And Peter, in true Memphis fashion,
we found friends.

00:13:26.814 --> 00:13:29.564
-They're gonna come in with us.
-Yeah. At the Duck Walk.

00:13:29.631 --> 00:13:31.110
How you doing, Kristen?

00:13:31.177 --> 00:13:33.473
Okay. So, we have the Walk of Fame here.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:37.618
Walk of stars.
They're gonna see Jerry Seinfeld tomorrow.

00:13:37.685 --> 00:13:39.593
-Seinfeld comes here tomorrow?
-Yes.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:41.187
Oh, fantastic.

00:13:41.254 --> 00:13:44.296
-This building's been here,
it'll be 100 years in 2028.

00:13:44.363 --> 00:13:47.319
[Bill] Coming to the South's finest theater

00:13:47.386 --> 00:13:50.586
that if you sat down on Broadway,
it would not look out of place.

00:13:50.653 --> 00:13:52.054
[Kristen] Thank you.

00:13:52.121 --> 00:13:54.967
So, this is our new season
that we just announced.

00:13:55.533 --> 00:13:57.872
[Peter] Oh, wow. This is beautiful.

00:13:58.549 --> 00:14:01.592
-There has been a theater
at this spot since 1890,

00:14:01.659 --> 00:14:05.594
but the building we're standing in
right now has been here since 1928.

00:14:05.661 --> 00:14:08.686
-I'll let you take it all in.
-[Peter] It is grand.

00:14:08.753 --> 00:14:12.897
You're not gonna see a lot of places
that have gold leaf, crystal chandeliers.

00:14:12.964 --> 00:14:14.753
-Oh, wow. Look at that balcony.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:16.966
Even the wide angle can't get it all in.

00:14:17.033 --> 00:14:19.392
-[Bill] Would you think this is in Memphis?
-No.

00:14:19.459 --> 00:14:21.916
-Nobody does.
-No. This is happening.

00:14:21.983 --> 00:14:23.812
So, do you guys pack this place out?

00:14:23.879 --> 00:14:27.181
Oh, yes. So, we're 2,300 seats.
We have a performance tomorrow.

00:14:27.248 --> 00:14:30.630
Our bread and butter is we bring
touring Broadway productions

00:14:30.697 --> 00:14:32.102
about eight times a year.

00:14:32.169 --> 00:14:36.079
But, of course, in between,
we have comedy, concerts, dance companies.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:38.477
-So, you're pretty fired up working here?
-Oh, yes.

00:14:38.544 --> 00:14:41.793
I'm a Broadway nerd
that gets to do what she loves every day,

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:44.352
which is introduce Broadway to Memphis.

00:14:44.753 --> 00:14:47.418
Which Memphis, I love.
I'm a born and raised Memphian.

00:14:47.485 --> 00:14:50.079
And then introduce Memphis
to the shows that I love.

00:14:50.146 --> 00:14:52.913
-Okay. As a born and raised Memphian…
-Yes.

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:54.795
-You love your city?
-I love my city.

00:14:54.862 --> 00:14:55.883
Why?

00:14:55.950 --> 00:15:00.333
-I love it because she's real honest
with herself, sometimes to a fault.

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:02.938
I think sometimes
we're really hard on ourselves.

00:15:03.005 --> 00:15:06.231
But Memphis is home. Memphis is family.
My entire family is here.

00:15:06.298 --> 00:15:08.577
And honestly, with me working here

00:15:08.644 --> 00:15:11.033
I always said I would leave
if I ever got bored.

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:14.594
But things are happening, growing,
changing at the Orpheum in Memphis,

00:15:14.661 --> 00:15:17.866
-So I love being here
-She's hard on herself. What do you mean?

00:15:17.933 --> 00:15:20.896
Memphis can be really critical
'cause we've let other people

00:15:20.963 --> 00:15:22.813
be critical of us without knowing us.

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:25.779
And so I think we can equally
be as hard on ourselves.

00:15:25.846 --> 00:15:28.350
-You've let other people write your story?
-Yeah.

00:15:28.417 --> 00:15:29.471
Absolutely.

00:15:29.538 --> 00:15:31.954
I feel very honored
to be able to work where I do

00:15:32.021 --> 00:15:34.500
in this crown jewel of Memphis.

00:15:34.567 --> 00:15:37.175
-This is stunning
as someone that loves architecture…

00:15:37.242 --> 00:15:39.872
-We're also one of
the most haunted places in Memphis.

00:15:39.939 --> 00:15:41.596
-You feel the haunted vibes here?

00:15:41.663 --> 00:15:45.308
-I haven't had any experiences,
but I've had some colleagues

00:15:45.375 --> 00:15:48.976
have some experiences,
and so I have to trust their experience.

00:15:49.043 --> 00:15:53.170
But that box right there is known to house
one of our most famous ghosts, Mary.

00:15:53.237 --> 00:15:55.037
-She's a 12-year-old ghost.
-Up here?

00:15:55.104 --> 00:15:57.334
Okay, right here. Guys, do you see Mary?

00:15:57.807 --> 00:16:00.407
-[Bill] What's the story?
-Coming through the screen?

00:16:00.474 --> 00:16:03.452
She was hit by a trolley car
outside the Orpheum,

00:16:03.519 --> 00:16:06.719
and she was brought inside,
and she loved it so much, she stayed.

00:16:06.786 --> 00:16:08.466
And so she's a mischievous ghost.

00:16:08.533 --> 00:16:11.603
Thankfully, we don't have any
malevolent spirits.

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:13.929
They're happy to be here. [laughs]

00:16:19.579 --> 00:16:21.782
[Peter] You know what
I don't see in Memphis?

00:16:21.849 --> 00:16:24.242
People set up on the streets,
other than Jackie.

00:16:24.309 --> 00:16:27.110
-Who seems like she's grandfathered in.
-She's a fixture.

00:16:27.177 --> 00:16:30.358
But I'm just saying,
I don't see people that…

00:16:30.425 --> 00:16:33.044
1,200 philanthropies
in the city of Memphis do.

00:16:33.111 --> 00:16:34.380
-Which isn't always good.

00:16:34.447 --> 00:16:35.976
People have a place to go.

00:16:36.696 --> 00:16:38.746
People have a place to go in many cities.

00:16:38.813 --> 00:16:42.563
People in Skid Row want to be on fentanyl,
don't want to go to those places,

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:44.380
'cause then they can't do fentanyl.

00:16:44.447 --> 00:16:46.691
Here, they must be enforcing it, as in,

00:16:46.758 --> 00:16:49.478
you don't have the option
to stay on the street, right?

00:16:49.545 --> 00:16:51.713
We don't have the police breaking up camps.

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:54.429
You also don't see cops, do you?

00:16:54.496 --> 00:16:56.403
I don't. I haven't seen a cop.

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:58.353
I hear a lot of sirens, I'm not gonna lie.

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:00.894
Near my place at night,
there was a lot of sirens,

00:17:00.961 --> 00:17:03.673
but I have not seen
any officers walking anywhere.

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:06.438
No, it's…
I'm just telling you, it's not a problem.

00:17:06.505 --> 00:17:07.968
It's not a big issue here.

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:11.471
There are so many churches downtown.

00:17:11.818 --> 00:17:14.723
We have more churches in Memphis
than we have gas stations.

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:16.610
Are people going to church?

00:17:16.677 --> 00:17:20.803
Well, the churches do outreach programs
where they have places for people to eat

00:17:20.870 --> 00:17:24.842
and to sleep, and the homeless
tend to congregate in those areas.

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:28.178
What about the Memphis Task Force
and the National Guard?

00:17:28.245 --> 00:17:32.453
A lot of people will know about that
'cause it hit mainstream media.

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:34.420
The National Guard is window dressing.

00:17:34.487 --> 00:17:35.840
What the Task Force is,

00:17:36.429 --> 00:17:39.117
is 17 entities

00:17:39.699 --> 00:17:43.965
from the federal government
all the way from Customs to the FBI

00:17:44.032 --> 00:17:46.226
to the Attorney General's Office

00:17:46.847 --> 00:17:50.480
that came to Memphis about six months ago
to work with the city

00:17:51.044 --> 00:17:55.760
to round up
its most dangerous felonious activity.

00:17:55.827 --> 00:17:56.714
All right?

00:17:56.781 --> 00:17:59.243
This is a Democrat city.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:04.308
This is the largest minority-majority city
in the United States, believe it or not.

00:18:04.375 --> 00:18:05.987
Not Atlanta, Memphis.

00:18:06.495 --> 00:18:08.678
And while policy-wise,

00:18:09.142 --> 00:18:13.268
there may be a lot of people
in government positions municipally

00:18:13.335 --> 00:18:18.232
that don't align with,
especially ICE and some other stuff,

00:18:18.823 --> 00:18:21.425
we can all agree
we needed help fighting crime.

00:18:21.492 --> 00:18:22.375
And so…

00:18:22.949 --> 00:18:25.908
much to the city government's credit,

00:18:26.761 --> 00:18:29.352
instead of fighting it,

00:18:30.646 --> 00:18:32.309
they said, look, it's inevitable.

00:18:32.376 --> 00:18:35.892
We can't keep the federal government
from coming here and doing work.

00:18:35.959 --> 00:18:38.481
But what we can do is we can fight it

00:18:38.548 --> 00:18:42.320
and we can have riots and the stuff
you've seen on TV in some other cities,

00:18:42.387 --> 00:18:46.700
or we can at least sit down at the table
and find places where we can agree.

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:50.740
And if we can do that
and build a relationship with each other,

00:18:50.807 --> 00:18:53.415
while we're not gonna support some of it,

00:18:53.860 --> 00:18:57.467
we can support part of it and get along.

00:18:57.534 --> 00:18:59.853
And at least we know what's going on.

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:03.020
And so that's the way
Memphis approached all of it.

00:19:03.087 --> 00:19:04.231
And as a result,

00:19:05.025 --> 00:19:06.488
crime is down this year.

00:19:06.555 --> 00:19:09.084
Violent crime is down, I think 42%.

00:19:09.151 --> 00:19:10.127
-Oh, wow.

00:19:10.194 --> 00:19:15.449
-We've had some very non-violent protests
against ICE, specifically.

00:19:15.943 --> 00:19:18.204
But beyond that,
I think by and large,

00:19:18.271 --> 00:19:22.263
the city has embraced
the fact that some FBI guys

00:19:22.330 --> 00:19:24.833
are rounding up felons
and the Highway Patrol

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:28.673
and the US Marshal Service interacting
with the Memphis Police Department

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.833
to get the worst off our streets.

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:34.809
-So are most people for it
or it just depends who you talk to?

00:19:34.876 --> 00:19:38.920
Oh, you know,
that's a hard question to answer

00:19:38.987 --> 00:19:41.139
because I don't know that it's been polled.

00:19:41.206 --> 00:19:43.136
And if it has, I don't know the polls.

00:19:43.203 --> 00:19:45.439
But I would tell you

00:19:46.022 --> 00:19:49.018
across racial and political spectrums,

00:19:49.085 --> 00:19:51.987
Black, White,
progressive, Democrat, whatever,

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:54.844
the vast majority of people that I talk to,

00:19:55.417 --> 00:19:59.123
will say there's good and bad
but the good outweighs the bad.

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:00.073
Okay.

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:03.644
And if it makes our city safer
and a better place to do business

00:20:03.711 --> 00:20:07.284
and a better place to go out to eat
and a better place to be entertained

00:20:07.351 --> 00:20:09.366
and a better place for our children,

00:20:09.433 --> 00:20:12.930
then I'll grin and bear
some of the parts I don't like

00:20:12.997 --> 00:20:15.203
for the good parts that have helped us.

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:20.574
And I really do think that's probably
the prevailing attitude toward it all.

00:20:21.019 --> 00:20:21.902
Gotcha.

00:20:23.473 --> 00:20:25.873
This is getting down near
the Mississippi, right?

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:27.786
Yeah. It's the river. This is front.

00:20:27.853 --> 00:20:30.836
It's typically considered
a business district.

00:20:30.903 --> 00:20:34.269
AutoZone was founded
by Pitt Hyde here in Memphis.

00:20:34.336 --> 00:20:38.603
And it's one of our bigger
publicly traded Memphis companies.

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:43.395
They've invested and their
world headquarters is Downtown, Memphis,

00:20:43.462 --> 00:20:46.223
which is right here on your left.

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:47.384
-Oh, there. Yep.

00:20:47.451 --> 00:20:49.236
-Very nice facility.

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:52.416
And what's neat is it's a confluence
of the old and new

00:20:52.483 --> 00:20:54.976
'cause you see all the old
architecture from…

00:20:55.043 --> 00:20:56.264
-Yeah, this is great.

00:20:56.331 --> 00:21:00.118
The first revival of the city
mixed with the new architecture

00:21:00.185 --> 00:21:02.116
from today's revival.

00:21:02.183 --> 00:21:05.563
The Cotton Museum is
the Cotton Exchange Building on the right.

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:09.943
That's where 80% of the world's cotton
was traded for many decades.

00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:12.935
-Okay. Out of this Art Deco beauty?
-That's Cotton Exchange Building.

00:21:13.002 --> 00:21:15.249
That's like the New York
Stock Exchange of cotton.

00:21:15.316 --> 00:21:16.199
-Okay.

00:21:16.266 --> 00:21:18.516
-Across the street
used to be a parking garage.

00:21:18.583 --> 00:21:20.692
That is now the new Brooks Museum,

00:21:20.759 --> 00:21:24.199
which you could put that thing
in New York or Chicago or LA

00:21:24.266 --> 00:21:25.873
and it would fit in just fine.

00:21:25.940 --> 00:21:28.006
-What kind of museum is it gonna be?
-Art.

00:21:28.073 --> 00:21:31.362
But what's amazing about it
is the floor plan.

00:21:31.429 --> 00:21:35.763
It's open and they're using
as homage to Memphis,

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:37.493
all kinds of hardwoods in it.

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:40.431
White oak flooring, locust walls.

00:21:40.866 --> 00:21:45.910
It's gonna have a massive promenade
out the backside overlooking the river.

00:21:46.450 --> 00:21:51.179
And then the top is an open air
gathering area on top of the museum.

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:54.664
We've had people from,
I think, Switzerland.

00:21:54.731 --> 00:21:59.237
I know New York,
have come to see this thing being built.

00:21:59.304 --> 00:22:01.436
-It's huge.
-All the natural wood products.

00:22:01.503 --> 00:22:04.793
It's huge and very, very, very impressive.

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:09.111
This was the custom house.
It was the customs office for

00:22:09.178 --> 00:22:12.673
when people came into our country
up the Mississippi River.

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:15.880
It was originally
US Customs and Border Patrol.

00:22:15.947 --> 00:22:18.463
It is now
the University of Memphis Law School.

00:22:18.530 --> 00:22:21.080
-A lot of immigrants
were coming up the Mississippi.

00:22:21.147 --> 00:22:23.447
-And their first touch on land was here?
-Lots.

00:22:23.514 --> 00:22:26.619
Some of those immigrants
were unfortunately slaves.

00:22:26.686 --> 00:22:28.213
-Right, right, right, right.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:31.561
So this was the customs house
that cleared both…

00:22:31.628 --> 00:22:34.180
-Oh, you're talking
all the way from Africa?

00:22:34.247 --> 00:22:36.350
Yes. Well, and the Caribbean.

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:38.213
-Wow.
-Yeah.

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:42.893
So New Orleans is the point where
the Mississippi River enters into the Gulf.

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:44.022
And you follow it up.

00:22:44.089 --> 00:22:46.986
And your next big stop
is Vicksburg, Mississippi.

00:22:47.053 --> 00:22:50.657
Then your next hub
was Memphis and then St. Louis.

00:22:50.731 --> 00:22:51.790
This is the trail.

00:22:52.430 --> 00:22:55.604
-Bill, where's all the money coming from
for a museum like that?

00:22:55.671 --> 00:22:58.393
-That's huge dollars.
-100% private donations.

00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:01.166
-So there's that much money
kicking around this town?

00:23:01.233 --> 00:23:03.251
There's a lot of old money in this town.

00:23:03.318 --> 00:23:05.538
But most importantly, the old money cares.

00:23:05.605 --> 00:23:10.323
The old money wants to get Memphis back
to what it rightfully should be.

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:12.855
There are people fighting to bring it back

00:23:12.922 --> 00:23:15.723
from what it went through
in the 60s and 70s.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:20.263
The reason it's called Memphis, it's on
the river like Memphis, Egypt on the Nile.

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:21.802
That's where it got its name.

00:23:21.868 --> 00:23:26.287
So some time ago, someone decided
we should build a pyramid

00:23:27.022 --> 00:23:28.473
because it's Memphis.

00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:31.745
So this was actually
where the University of Memphis

00:23:31.812 --> 00:23:33.223
and the Grizzlies played.

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:34.918
-It was a sports arena.
-Okay.

00:23:34.985 --> 00:23:37.503
And it was the Memphis Pyramid.
It's on the river.

00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:40.167
And anyway, it became outdated.

00:23:40.234 --> 00:23:42.756
It was really hard to outfit
for more seats.

00:23:42.823 --> 00:23:46.416
And believe it or not,
Bass Pro bought the thing.

00:23:46.483 --> 00:23:49.215
And it's now the largest
Bass Pro in the United States.

00:23:49.289 --> 00:23:52.508
But what's interesting is
inside the bottom of it,

00:23:52.575 --> 00:23:54.843
you're walking through
a massive zoo aquarium.

00:23:54.910 --> 00:23:56.720
There's alligators and massive fish.

00:23:56.787 --> 00:23:58.653
-No way.
-Oh, it's incredible.

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:02.839
And inside it is the largest
freestanding elevator in North America

00:24:02.906 --> 00:24:04.953
that takes you to the tip top of that.

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:06.634
You can get out there and watch.

00:24:06.701 --> 00:24:09.000
And inside that glass thing up there

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:12.521
is some massive four-story aquarium.

00:24:12.588 --> 00:24:16.303
That's like 30 feet in diameter,
full of all kind of catfish and stuff.

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:17.488
And it's very cool.

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:21.683
Not only four miles
from all of the things you just saw

00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:26.781
are also neighborhoods that are
in dire need of investment and repair.

00:24:26.848 --> 00:24:28.481
And the city is working on it.

00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:32.379
But it is where my players
from Manassas came from.

00:24:32.446 --> 00:24:34.273
-It is…
-Okay, just to explain that.

00:24:34.340 --> 00:24:36.763
-You were a high school football coach.
-Yeah.

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:39.397
You made a fantastic documentary.

00:24:39.464 --> 00:24:41.943
I was minding my own business
coaching football.

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:46.237
And two dudes that looked like you with
skinny legged jeans, they wore scarves.

00:24:46.304 --> 00:24:48.204
-Are they skinny?
-Yeah, they had hair.

00:24:48.271 --> 00:24:50.776
But they wore scarves, showed up from LA,

00:24:50.843 --> 00:24:53.300
and followed us around and made a movie

00:24:53.367 --> 00:24:59.053
that we thought we'd see on Channel 522
at 3:00 AM one Wednesday night.

00:24:59.120 --> 00:25:00.378
And it went big.

00:25:00.445 --> 00:25:03.318
And yeah, 10 months later
I'm walking down the red carpet

00:25:03.385 --> 00:25:05.719
at the Academy Awards
and it won an Oscar.

00:25:05.786 --> 00:25:08.223
-Back in 2011?
-Yeah. It's called Undefeated.

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:10.766
And it was about me coaching football.

00:25:10.833 --> 00:25:14.935
And the movie's not about football,
it's a human interest story.

00:25:15.002 --> 00:25:15.885
-It's so good.

00:25:15.953 --> 00:25:19.513
[Bills voice] The character of a man
is not measured in how he handles his wins

00:25:19.580 --> 00:25:22.353
but what he does with his failures,
and tonight we failed.

00:25:22.938 --> 00:25:26.514
And everybody says
when you get these inner city kids down,

00:25:26.581 --> 00:25:28.731
they'll lay over
and you'll beat them by 40.

00:25:28.798 --> 00:25:29.681
Not us!

00:25:30.260 --> 00:25:31.696
You walk with your chins up.

00:25:31.763 --> 00:25:34.483
I am proud to be a Manassas Tiger
on this field tonight

00:25:34.550 --> 00:25:36.613
'cause my guys didn't lay down.

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:37.673
They got heart.

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:40.340
Now let's put our mind
with our heart and our bodies.

00:25:40.407 --> 00:25:42.723
And let's finish this thing this year.

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:46.969
Those kids that I coached at Manassas
are also part of the Memphis story.

00:25:47.453 --> 00:25:51.798
And the work that we've done downtown
and continue downtown to bring it up,

00:25:51.865 --> 00:25:54.816
same in midtown
that we'll show you in a little while.

00:25:54.883 --> 00:25:57.386
We have to have the same level
of care and concern

00:25:57.453 --> 00:26:00.523
for the most disadvantaged
among us that exist in Memphis.

00:26:00.590 --> 00:26:03.773
And I think it's important
to see all sides of a cube.

00:26:03.840 --> 00:26:04.723
-Yep.

00:26:04.790 --> 00:26:08.040
And part of the Memphis cube is
we still have a lot of work to do.

00:26:08.107 --> 00:26:11.638
And where it came from largely
has to do with the White flight

00:26:11.705 --> 00:26:16.021
that started after the King assassination
and busing in the early 70s.

00:26:16.088 --> 00:26:20.398
-So White flight happened because crime
ticked up and families of means were like,

00:26:20.465 --> 00:26:23.615
-"I don't want to raise my kids in this."
-Some of that's right.

00:26:23.682 --> 00:26:27.573
And also some of it's because White people
were scared of Black children.

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:28.883
I mean, it's just true.

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:32.815
I don't want my White kids
to go to school with Black kids.

00:26:32.882 --> 00:26:35.485
-When integration was happening?
-Yes. That's busing.

00:26:35.552 --> 00:26:38.123
When you take kids
out of an all-Black community

00:26:38.190 --> 00:26:41.738
and bus them to an White community
and you take kids out of a White community

00:26:41.805 --> 00:26:44.859
and bus them to an all-Black community
in the name of integration,

00:26:44.926 --> 00:26:46.073
that sounds right.

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:48.279
But the people with wealth,

00:26:48.346 --> 00:26:51.136
they just take their ball and go home.

00:26:51.203 --> 00:26:54.953
The people without wealth that don't
like that plan, they've got no options.

00:26:55.020 --> 00:26:56.807
And so what happens is

00:26:56.874 --> 00:26:58.910
you end up with White flight

00:26:59.408 --> 00:27:03.960
and all of the resources and access
that goes along with business ownership

00:27:04.027 --> 00:27:07.998
and everything else that leave
and it creates an access desert.

00:27:08.495 --> 00:27:11.578
A lot of people talk about
food deserts, academic deserts.

00:27:11.645 --> 00:27:13.253
I call it access deserts.

00:27:13.670 --> 00:27:16.109
You have two or three generations of that,

00:27:16.176 --> 00:27:18.544
and you end up with blight.

00:27:18.611 --> 00:27:21.252
You end up with schools
not having private investment

00:27:21.319 --> 00:27:23.681
and PTA clubs and booster clubs.

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:27.136
And you end up with disenfranchisement

00:27:27.203 --> 00:27:30.122
and gangs and crime

00:27:30.189 --> 00:27:31.613
fills the vacuum.

00:27:32.126 --> 00:27:33.725
It's two tales of one city,

00:27:34.196 --> 00:27:36.016
instead of a tale of two cities.

00:27:36.083 --> 00:27:39.043
Part of the Memphis story
is a story of loss.

00:27:39.110 --> 00:27:42.557
While we still have FedEx,
and International Paper, and AutoZone,

00:27:42.624 --> 00:27:45.996
and Terminex, and ServiceMaster,
and a lot of great companies,

00:27:46.063 --> 00:27:47.373
we lost Firestone.

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:49.105
We lost Caterpillar.

00:27:49.172 --> 00:27:50.328
We lost Northwest.

00:27:50.395 --> 00:27:51.893
We were a Northwest hub.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:54.278
And when Delta bought them,
we lost our hub.

00:27:54.345 --> 00:27:56.762
And every time one of those things lose,

00:27:56.829 --> 00:28:00.675
goes with it,
good blue-collar jobs typically.

00:28:01.069 --> 00:28:04.219
-Firestone Dental,
so you can see the footprint of it here.

00:28:04.286 --> 00:28:06.486
Oh, you're about to
really see the footprint.

00:28:06.553 --> 00:28:08.468
This is what is called New Chicago.

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:11.415
Once, up until the 70s,

00:28:11.991 --> 00:28:14.658
an extremely proud

00:28:15.128 --> 00:28:16.963
Black neighborhood.

00:28:17.030 --> 00:28:19.208
Isaac Hayes went to high school here.

00:28:19.275 --> 00:28:21.193
Great schools, great everything.

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:23.938
This 170 acres to our left

00:28:24.005 --> 00:28:26.955
was the second largest
Firestone plant in the United States.

00:28:27.022 --> 00:28:27.905
-Okay.

00:28:27.972 --> 00:28:31.108
And when it left,
all of these businesses on the right…

00:28:31.175 --> 00:28:33.299
-You see these abandoned smokestacks?
-Yep.

00:28:33.366 --> 00:28:34.713
It wasn't just Firestone.

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:38.998
It was all the smaller industries
around Firestone that supported Firestone.

00:28:39.065 --> 00:28:41.610
It's like if you got a Ford Motor Company,

00:28:41.677 --> 00:28:44.853
you've also got a seat manufacturer
and a bolt manufacturer.

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:47.450
And Firestone leaves,
you leave all these others.

00:28:47.517 --> 00:28:50.214
These were massive industrial complexes

00:28:50.281 --> 00:28:52.493
employing thousands of people.

00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:54.737
Look how all the way back
to the power grid.

00:28:54.804 --> 00:28:57.354
-All the way back there?
-We've been driving a mile.

00:28:57.421 --> 00:28:59.197
That was one Firestone plant.

00:28:59.264 --> 00:29:01.704
-Okay.
So this part of town was thriving.

00:29:01.771 --> 00:29:05.236
-50s, 60s?
-It was. Absolutely. Blue collar, thriving.

00:29:05.303 --> 00:29:08.598
Grocery stores, restaurants,
businesses, housing.

00:29:08.665 --> 00:29:11.513
-No food desert at that time?
-No. It was great.

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:15.698
But the problem is
the people who can afford to be mobile,

00:29:16.252 --> 00:29:17.346
they leave.

00:29:17.413 --> 00:29:19.616
They vote with their wallet and their feet.

00:29:19.683 --> 00:29:21.063
-Just like anyone would.

00:29:21.130 --> 00:29:22.711
Just like anyone would.

00:29:22.778 --> 00:29:26.195
The people who can't,
who don't have mobility,

00:29:26.262 --> 00:29:29.144
who don't have that access
to mobility, they stay.

00:29:29.625 --> 00:29:33.643
And then three generations later,
we wonder why their kids are in gangs

00:29:33.710 --> 00:29:35.781
and not doing well in school.

00:29:35.848 --> 00:29:38.789
Well, what have they had access to
other than loss?

00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:42.476
What have they had access to
other than blight?

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:43.993
Right?

00:29:44.061 --> 00:29:48.031
And there has not been enough investment.

00:29:48.098 --> 00:29:49.647
And when I say investment,

00:29:49.714 --> 00:29:54.052
there's all kinds of 501(c)
and all that stuff that spend money.

00:29:54.490 --> 00:29:56.220
I'm talking about real investment.

00:29:56.287 --> 00:29:59.405
Investment in the people.
Investment in the education.

00:29:59.472 --> 00:30:02.135
-Similar tale in other parts
of the country, you know?

00:30:02.202 --> 00:30:05.508
You can go deep into Appalachia,
into West Virginia, right?

00:30:05.575 --> 00:30:08.763
The coal jobs, a lot of them have left.
Things are down and out.

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:10.598
But dude, this is gangland.

00:30:10.665 --> 00:30:11.665
This is gangland?

00:30:11.732 --> 00:30:13.230
Oh, this is gangland.

00:30:13.297 --> 00:30:15.978
And I say that without hesitation

00:30:16.045 --> 00:30:19.613
because I can point at houses
where my former players lived.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:22.795
I've been all over these streets
rounding up kids.

00:30:22.862 --> 00:30:24.393
O.C. Brown from the movie?

00:30:24.460 --> 00:30:26.660
That's his grandmama's house,
the yellow one.

00:30:26.727 --> 00:30:28.843
-Yeah, that's the guy in your movie.

00:30:28.910 --> 00:30:31.513
You guys got to see the documentary.
It's so cool.

00:30:31.580 --> 00:30:34.943
-This house right here
is where one of my tailbacks lived.

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:37.086
They were brothers, Mark and Larry.

00:30:37.153 --> 00:30:39.073
Jamie Bobo lived around the corner.

00:30:39.140 --> 00:30:41.964
But if you look,
these are for the gotten people.

00:30:43.090 --> 00:30:45.332
-But look at that right there.
-Yeah.

00:30:45.399 --> 00:30:46.854
New playground, right?

00:30:46.921 --> 00:30:49.881
-Yeah, there was a couple of kids
killed there not long ago.

00:30:49.948 --> 00:30:51.207
-Oh, geez.
-Gunfire.

00:30:51.640 --> 00:30:53.581
Notice that there's nobody in it.

00:30:54.412 --> 00:30:57.751
You can build a park all you want
but that's not investing.

00:30:57.818 --> 00:30:59.743
-But money isn't the solution.
-No.

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:01.960
It's part of it, but it's not the solution.

00:31:02.027 --> 00:31:05.909
We got to invest in the people, education.

00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:08.708
-So how does it feel for you
coming through here?

00:31:08.775 --> 00:31:11.676
-Just kind of like old home week.
It hadn't changed.

00:31:11.743 --> 00:31:14.111
This is what it looked like 20 years ago.

00:31:14.178 --> 00:31:16.378
And look at them.
They're planting something.

00:31:16.445 --> 00:31:18.203
It's a neighborhood garden.

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:20.533
They're gonna be growing vegetables.

00:31:20.610 --> 00:31:23.806
So it takes everyone in the community
and that's the hard part.

00:31:23.873 --> 00:31:27.173
That's why it's so complicated
because you got to go with the kids.

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:29.358
Most people my age are not changing.

00:31:29.833 --> 00:31:31.546
Right? It's just, let's be honest.

00:31:31.613 --> 00:31:33.371
So you got to go with the kids.

00:31:33.820 --> 00:31:37.292
But then to give the kids the best chance
they need good parents.

00:31:37.359 --> 00:31:41.886
So that does also mean
you have to go at parental advice.

00:31:41.953 --> 00:31:45.133
Do you know what the data says
about reading proficiency?

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:47.200
-It's pretty bad right now, right?

00:31:47.943 --> 00:31:48.884
Here's the data.

00:31:49.670 --> 00:31:53.233
If you don't read
on grade level by third grade

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:57.480
you are 70% more likely to be locked up

00:31:58.203 --> 00:32:02.683
and divorced than someone who does
read on grade level by third grade.

00:32:03.190 --> 00:32:05.618
So then you take an area like this

00:32:06.037 --> 00:32:10.229
where tons of single parent households
are working all kinds of shifts.

00:32:10.296 --> 00:32:12.363
Kids aren't being read nighttime stories.

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:14.369
They aren't being sung lullabies.

00:32:14.436 --> 00:32:17.041
And by and large,
their parents weren't either.

00:32:17.108 --> 00:32:21.132
What you find is
you find a huge literacy gap

00:32:21.958 --> 00:32:26.859
that starts the downhill boulder
that the data says

00:32:27.249 --> 00:32:30.545
you can do all the work you want to
when people are 21 years old.

00:32:30.612 --> 00:32:32.492
-But the damage has been done.
-Yeah.

00:32:32.559 --> 00:32:34.303
We have to invest in the schools.

00:32:34.370 --> 00:32:36.678
We have to invest in early education.

00:32:36.745 --> 00:32:39.070
We have to invest in…

00:32:39.137 --> 00:32:41.196
This is piggyback on what you said.

00:32:41.703 --> 00:32:44.353
You're right.
People our age aren't gonna do anything.

00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:48.163
We've got to invest in these kids
so they can change this for themselves.

00:32:48.230 --> 00:32:49.901
We don't need to be paternalistic

00:32:49.968 --> 00:32:53.301
and tell everybody
what they need for their neighborhoods.

00:32:53.368 --> 00:32:56.073
We've got to invest in the children
in their neighborhoods.

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:59.023
In the literacy programs, in business,

00:32:59.090 --> 00:33:01.503
and let them work on
their own neighborhoods.

00:33:01.570 --> 00:33:03.386
-Here's the reality too, Bill.

00:33:03.453 --> 00:33:04.749
I'm all over the country.

00:33:04.816 --> 00:33:07.936
You know who I'm meeting a lot of
these days? What demographic?

00:33:08.003 --> 00:33:10.176
Former teachers that aren't that old.

00:33:10.243 --> 00:33:12.003
They're not at retirement age.

00:33:12.070 --> 00:33:14.353
They're getting out of the profession. Why?

00:33:14.420 --> 00:33:15.965
It's gotten really hard. Why?

00:33:16.032 --> 00:33:20.536
Because they can't discipline like
they need to to keep class in order. Why?

00:33:20.603 --> 00:33:23.143
What support
are they getting from the parents?

00:33:23.210 --> 00:33:26.089
Exactly.
And so the parents aren't doing their work.

00:33:26.156 --> 00:33:27.318
Many of them at home.

00:33:27.385 --> 00:33:29.464
And so they drop their kids off.

00:33:30.290 --> 00:33:33.215
And then drop
a chaotic situation on the teachers

00:33:33.282 --> 00:33:35.132
and the teachers can't do anything.

00:33:35.199 --> 00:33:37.823
And they're like, you know what?
Why am I doing this?

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:40.419
And they're leaving.
A lot of good teachers.

00:33:41.229 --> 00:33:42.932
-Yeah.
-So the problem goes deep.

00:33:42.999 --> 00:33:44.903
And we're not gonna have the solution.

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:46.831
-What is this?
-I have no idea.

00:33:46.898 --> 00:33:48.963
Let's go check this out.

00:33:50.588 --> 00:33:51.763
[Peter] How you guys doing?

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:54.301
-You got a trucking school out here?
-Yeah.

00:33:55.330 --> 00:33:58.103
-You guys are all getting your licenses?
-Yes.

00:33:58.170 --> 00:34:00.503
-So you do your training right here?
-Yes, sir.

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:02.789
-You're gonna truck?
-Yeah. I'm 19 years old.

00:34:02.856 --> 00:34:05.276
-19, you ready to hit the open road?
-Yes, sir.

00:34:05.343 --> 00:34:07.702
Nice.
It's a good way to see the country, right?

00:34:07.769 --> 00:34:08.958
For sure.

00:34:09.025 --> 00:34:11.217
-Get paid to travel.
-See new things, too.

00:34:11.284 --> 00:34:14.517
Okay. What's the pay right now
if you get a good gig,

00:34:14.584 --> 00:34:16.244
cross-country trucking?

00:34:16.311 --> 00:34:18.138
Best case scenario, what can you make?

00:34:18.205 --> 00:34:20.818
-$100,000.
-$100,000?

00:34:20.885 --> 00:34:22.767
-Yeah. $100,000 a year.
-Nice.

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:25.921
How is it right now
instructing the young guys?

00:34:25.988 --> 00:34:29.247
It's rewarding watching people changing.

00:34:29.314 --> 00:34:31.586
-So it's a great opportunity?
-For sure.

00:34:32.160 --> 00:34:33.590
It could be life-changing.

00:34:34.070 --> 00:34:36.950
Within two years,
you can get $100,000 a year trucking.

00:34:37.017 --> 00:34:40.298
And you're not spending much, right?
Living on the road?

00:34:40.365 --> 00:34:43.318
That depends if you drive
for a company or you own and operate

00:34:43.385 --> 00:34:47.151
If you own and operate you gonna be
responsible for all your own expenses.

00:34:47.219 --> 00:34:48.102
Insurance.

00:34:48.643 --> 00:34:49.637
Your authority.

00:34:50.380 --> 00:34:51.958
 All the repairs on your truck.

00:34:52.025 --> 00:34:54.703
But if you drive for somebody else,
you just drive.

00:34:54.770 --> 00:34:56.830
-You guys all grew up in Memphis?
-Yeah.

00:34:56.897 --> 00:34:58.490
-Do you love your city?
-Yeah.

00:34:58.557 --> 00:34:59.463
Love it?

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:02.638
You weren't scared to
just come out here and pull up like this?

00:35:02.705 --> 00:35:03.881
-I'm not. Should I be?

00:35:03.948 --> 00:35:05.069
Nah.

00:35:05.136 --> 00:35:06.803
No? I go everywhere.

00:35:08.097 --> 00:35:12.173
I've been in the hoods
of Karachi, Pakistan, Saudi Arabia, Iran.

00:35:12.240 --> 00:35:16.501
I just feel it out. If I felt a bad vibe,
I would have went the other way.

00:35:16.906 --> 00:35:19.353
Don't look at the stereotypes.
You just got to be here to see it.

00:35:19.793 --> 00:35:21.087
Take care of you guys.

00:35:22.416 --> 00:35:23.651
Those guys are great.

00:35:24.260 --> 00:35:26.228
I knew you'd love those guys.

00:35:26.295 --> 00:35:28.721
They're calling you
a crazy White boy right now.

00:35:28.788 --> 00:35:32.207
Yeah. They said, weren't you scared
to come over? I just feel it out.

00:35:32.274 --> 00:35:35.982
If I felt a bad energy, I would have
turned the other way but I didn't.

00:35:36.049 --> 00:35:38.649
There's nothing to be scared of.
They're just people.

00:35:38.716 --> 00:35:42.166
At first they're like, who's the White cop
looking dude with a camera?

00:35:42.233 --> 00:35:44.945
-Yeah.
-And you have to sort of prove yourself.

00:35:45.012 --> 00:35:48.219
But it's cool. So those guys,
they're getting their CDL licenses.

00:35:48.286 --> 00:35:49.169
Right.

00:35:49.236 --> 00:35:51.876
So you're coming out of here
potentially making 100K.

00:35:51.943 --> 00:35:53.822
-It's huge.
-It's huge.

00:35:53.889 --> 00:35:58.111
And I don't want to get dark with this,
but I'm just gonna say what I feel.

00:35:58.178 --> 00:35:59.061
-Yeah.

00:35:59.128 --> 00:36:02.723
Imagine the day when
automated trucking just takes over.

00:36:02.790 --> 00:36:04.439
You know, when that day comes.

00:36:04.506 --> 00:36:06.425
Maybe there's another opportunity

00:36:06.492 --> 00:36:09.518
but that one's gonna close down
for guys like this here.

00:36:09.585 --> 00:36:12.033
I wonder and worry about a lot of that too.

00:36:12.643 --> 00:36:15.792
Think how many people make a living
driving a taxi and an Uber.

00:36:15.859 --> 00:36:17.406
-Yeah.
-All of it.

00:36:17.473 --> 00:36:20.237
You were literally two miles from
where we were just at.

00:36:20.304 --> 00:36:22.578
-Different universe.
-A different universe.

00:36:22.645 --> 00:36:24.013
-The Orpheum and here.

00:36:24.080 --> 00:36:26.198
Literally two miles from each other.

00:36:26.910 --> 00:36:28.894
The hood is pretty big in Memphis?

00:36:28.961 --> 00:36:31.861
-The hood is big in Memphis.
-Both on north and south side?

00:36:31.928 --> 00:36:35.853
You got to think of Memphis geographically.
If you go west, you're in Arkansas.

00:36:35.920 --> 00:36:39.370
Well, you're the river, right?
If you go south, you're in Mississippi.

00:36:39.437 --> 00:36:43.168
So we're in the very southwestern most
corner of Tennessee.

00:36:43.235 --> 00:36:47.019
So the only place to go is north and east.

00:36:47.419 --> 00:36:51.276
As you think about the city,
the downtown's the center.

00:36:51.343 --> 00:36:55.333
Then the central of the city
is midtown out through East Memphis.

00:36:55.400 --> 00:36:59.350
The hoods are South Memphis
and North Memphis.

00:36:59.773 --> 00:37:02.623
So the hoods surround the city corridor.

00:37:02.690 --> 00:37:04.516
Geographically, that's how it works.

00:37:04.583 --> 00:37:06.243
Now, you cannot…

00:37:06.683 --> 00:37:10.274
you simply cannot tell the story of Memphis

00:37:10.341 --> 00:37:12.664
without including St. Jude Hospital.

00:37:13.460 --> 00:37:16.392
St. Jude Hospital
was founded by Danny Thomas,

00:37:16.459 --> 00:37:19.493
who back in the 50s and 60s
was a megastar, right?

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:21.919
Danny Thomas talk show, Danny Thomas this,

00:37:21.986 --> 00:37:24.585
Sammy Davis Jr.,
Danny Thomas, all those guys.

00:37:24.652 --> 00:37:25.716
He's from Memphis.

00:37:25.783 --> 00:37:30.016
He started out on a quest
to cure childhood cancer.

00:37:30.083 --> 00:37:34.689
Back when children who got cancer,
92 or 93% of children died.

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:38.870
That number is now like 17%.

00:37:39.683 --> 00:37:44.120
And he started St. Jude Children's
Research Hospital for Cancer Research,

00:37:44.187 --> 00:37:46.996
which is these pink buildings
are part of the campus.

00:37:47.063 --> 00:37:48.666
-Okay, those two big buildings?

00:37:48.733 --> 00:37:49.742
That's two of them.

00:37:49.809 --> 00:37:52.107
The campus is now 60 acres.

00:37:52.174 --> 00:37:55.407
It is the leading
children's research hospital in the world.

00:37:55.843 --> 00:37:58.182
And nobody pays a dime.

00:37:58.570 --> 00:37:59.753
-No way.

00:37:59.820 --> 00:38:03.883
-We have children in Memphis
from all over the world

00:38:03.950 --> 00:38:08.742
with the rarest,
sickest type of childhood cancers.

00:38:08.809 --> 00:38:13.626
And they come here
and their lodging, their care,

00:38:13.693 --> 00:38:14.828
zero dollars.

00:38:14.894 --> 00:38:16.824
-Who's paying for it?
-Donations.

00:38:16.891 --> 00:38:18.593
It's a private entity.

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:21.071
-So donations are paying
the doctors' salaries?

00:38:21.138 --> 00:38:24.497
-And this entire campus
that goes down three blocks.

00:38:24.564 --> 00:38:25.447
-Whoa.

00:38:26.414 --> 00:38:28.537
All of what you see is St. Jude.

00:38:28.604 --> 00:38:30.313
-This comes right out of the hood.

00:38:30.380 --> 00:38:31.909
You were just in the hood.

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:34.515
You were in the hood a minute ago.

00:38:34.582 --> 00:38:35.549
And now look.

00:38:35.616 --> 00:38:36.499
-Okay, so this…

00:38:36.566 --> 00:38:39.244
This is the reinvestment
in Memphis I'm talking about.

00:38:39.311 --> 00:38:41.343
-Look at this place.
-Yeah, that's crazy.

00:38:41.410 --> 00:38:42.619
Dude, do you understand?

00:38:42.686 --> 00:38:44.957
They are treating 600 children right now

00:38:45.024 --> 00:38:48.260
for the rarest forms
of childhood cancer known to mankind.

00:38:48.327 --> 00:38:53.197
And nobody that is in that hospital
is paying a dollar for that care.

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:57.776
And they, St. Jude's,
every time they make a breakthrough,

00:38:58.140 --> 00:39:01.326
they don't try to publicize it
or keep it as their own.

00:39:01.393 --> 00:39:05.344
They share 100% of all of their research

00:39:05.411 --> 00:39:07.626
with every children's hospital on earth.

00:39:07.693 --> 00:39:09.493
-How does one get their kid in there?

00:39:09.560 --> 00:39:13.479
You call and say, my kid's got cancer
and if they've got a bed, you're in.

00:39:14.110 --> 00:39:19.194
And there's kids here from Argentina,
from Ukraine, from Memphis.

00:39:19.261 --> 00:39:21.340
There's kids here from all over the world.

00:39:21.407 --> 00:39:25.393
Okay, but check this out. The
opportunities are right next to the hood.

00:39:25.848 --> 00:39:27.057
Yes. All over the place.

00:39:27.124 --> 00:39:29.812
-They're not out in the middle of nowhere.
-No!

00:39:29.879 --> 00:39:32.060
A high-paying job
is literally out the door.

00:39:32.127 --> 00:39:35.161
-Look at all this development.
-No, but hear me out, Bill.

00:39:35.228 --> 00:39:37.896
-But if you're not educated on how to work…
-Exactly.

00:39:37.963 --> 00:39:39.714
That's what I was trying to get at.

00:39:39.781 --> 00:39:42.732
You can literally walk out your door
to a high-paying job,

00:39:42.799 --> 00:39:45.510
but if you don't have the skills,
you're not making it.

00:39:45.577 --> 00:39:48.286
And some guy from India
is flying in and working there.

00:39:48.353 --> 00:39:49.236
Yes.

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:51.533
[Bill] It is unbelievable.

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:54.150
Everything you see is the hospital.

00:39:54.217 --> 00:39:56.100
And people cross the bridge,

00:39:56.571 --> 00:39:59.719
drive past it every day, and have no idea.

00:40:00.163 --> 00:40:01.046
-I had no idea.

00:40:02.270 --> 00:40:05.753
So, as we kind of transfer out of downtown,

00:40:05.820 --> 00:40:09.071
heading east toward what we call midtown,

00:40:09.138 --> 00:40:10.523
it's still the same vibe.

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:13.243
The center corridor is full of development.

00:40:13.310 --> 00:40:16.170
The north and the south
need continual work.

00:40:16.650 --> 00:40:20.257
One of the more iconic places in Midtown
is up here on your left,

00:40:20.964 --> 00:40:22.537
which is Sun Studios.

00:40:23.220 --> 00:40:26.625
Which is Jerry Lee Lewis, Roy Orbison,

00:40:26.692 --> 00:40:28.863
Elvis Presley, B.B. King.

00:40:29.413 --> 00:40:31.429
Johnny Cash recorded his albums here.

00:40:31.496 --> 00:40:32.379
No way.

00:40:32.446 --> 00:40:36.100
Yeah, that's Sun Studios.
That's where all of that music was made.

00:40:36.167 --> 00:40:37.050
Right there.

00:40:37.540 --> 00:40:40.115
The home of blues
and rock and roll is Memphis.

00:40:40.182 --> 00:40:42.218
And the home of country is Nashville.

00:40:42.285 --> 00:40:45.924
-Tennessee is responsible
for so much music. Isn't that crazy?

00:40:45.991 --> 00:40:48.144
-Tennessee is responsible
for so much music.

00:40:48.211 --> 00:40:50.783
-I never thought about it like that.
-It's true.

00:40:50.860 --> 00:40:53.478
-This is Overton Square. Pretty cool area.
-Okay.

00:40:53.545 --> 00:40:57.920
This was the first place in Tennessee
you could buy liquor by the glass.

00:40:58.600 --> 00:41:00.525
And a place you may have heard of,

00:41:00.592 --> 00:41:03.731
where the very first one
was right here where this balcony was.

00:41:03.798 --> 00:41:07.275
It was an interesting place
and they didn't know what to name it until

00:41:07.342 --> 00:41:10.452
a guy got a drink by a glass and said,
"Thank God it's Friday."

00:41:10.520 --> 00:41:12.047
-TGI Fridays.
-Yeah.

00:41:12.114 --> 00:41:13.933
-Right there.
-Okay. There you go.

00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:16.354
This is now what's considered Midtown,

00:41:16.421 --> 00:41:19.373
but in the 20s,
this was the outskirts, right?

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:21.420
Only three miles from here

00:41:21.487 --> 00:41:24.607
is the third most dangerous zip code
in the United States.

00:41:24.674 --> 00:41:26.316
-Yeah. That's wild.
-Right.

00:41:26.383 --> 00:41:28.765
But this is called Central Gardens,

00:41:28.832 --> 00:41:33.823
which was the first kind of affluent
development outside of downtown.

00:41:33.890 --> 00:41:36.161
It is still very, very well regarded.

00:41:36.600 --> 00:41:38.763
Beautiful houses, antebellum.

00:41:38.830 --> 00:41:43.373
There's a lot of energy here
in terms of keeping the neighborhood up

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:46.739
and pretty and well maintained.

00:41:46.806 --> 00:41:50.467
-So what are people doing for work here now
to afford these places?

00:41:50.534 --> 00:41:52.044
Or it's just old money?

00:41:52.111 --> 00:41:54.281
-We still have a massive banking community.

00:41:54.348 --> 00:41:56.473
Still huge in transportation.

00:41:56.540 --> 00:41:58.662
There's tons of industry in Memphis still.

00:41:58.729 --> 00:42:01.889
-Okay. How much are some of those homes
we just passed, roughly?

00:42:01.956 --> 00:42:03.488
-Here? A million dollars.

00:42:03.555 --> 00:42:05.023
$800,000 to a million.

00:42:05.090 --> 00:42:08.596
You go down here
and they're a $1.5, $2 million.

00:42:08.663 --> 00:42:10.343
-Okay.
-So it's pretty stately.

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:12.056
Beautiful old trees in here.

00:42:13.115 --> 00:42:14.933
And kids walk to school here.

00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:16.889
Still ride their bikes to school.

00:42:16.956 --> 00:42:20.753
You know, this big, bad, dangerous area
where our kids walk to school.

00:42:20.820 --> 00:42:25.125
-So when these people see on the news,
like, Memphis is failing…

00:42:25.192 --> 00:42:27.709
These are people
that are digging in and investing.

00:42:27.776 --> 00:42:29.728
These people could live in the suburbs.

00:42:29.795 --> 00:42:32.245
They can buy anything
they want to in the suburbs.

00:42:32.312 --> 00:42:36.173
But they choose to put down roots
and support their community.

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:39.365
-Well, and also the suburbs
aren't gonna make a home like this.

00:42:39.432 --> 00:42:42.961
No. But there are some suburbs
that are pretty insanely nice.

00:42:43.028 --> 00:42:46.245
-Okay. So the big, big money of Memphis,
where is it?

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:50.154
-It's in the center of the city,
out through the suburbs.

00:42:53.048 --> 00:42:55.048
All right, guys. Bill had to take a call,

00:42:55.115 --> 00:42:57.459
so I got a few minutes to walk around here

00:42:57.986 --> 00:42:59.769
just to show you what it's like.

00:43:00.272 --> 00:43:04.005
These absolutely
stunningly beautiful homes.

00:43:05.793 --> 00:43:06.911
How you doing, man?

00:43:08.073 --> 00:43:09.306
Ladies beware?

00:43:09.373 --> 00:43:10.256
Yeah.

00:43:10.638 --> 00:43:12.939
Not guys. [laughs]

00:43:14.299 --> 00:43:17.665
Yeah, guys. So when you go down
streets like this in Memphis,

00:43:18.086 --> 00:43:20.345
which there are actually more of them

00:43:20.412 --> 00:43:24.448
than I thought there would be,
it's just, I don't know what? Mayberry?

00:43:25.073 --> 00:43:27.956
Really blissful, nice, beautiful.

00:43:28.023 --> 00:43:30.199
Things well taken care of, obviously.

00:43:30.840 --> 00:43:32.664
And then you saw the other side

00:43:33.437 --> 00:43:35.653
of the tracks
and the story for that, right?

00:43:35.720 --> 00:43:39.613
Firestone going out of business
and the companies that went with it.

00:43:39.680 --> 00:43:43.104
It's a complicated city
for being under a million people.

00:43:43.171 --> 00:43:46.493
It's still, you know,
substantial size, over 600,000.

00:43:46.560 --> 00:43:49.595
And I'm sure there are
some middle class neighborhoods

00:43:49.662 --> 00:43:52.093
that are in between what we've seen today.

00:43:52.180 --> 00:43:54.004
But this just goes to show you,

00:43:54.528 --> 00:43:57.066
easy labels are just that, easy labels.

00:43:57.133 --> 00:44:01.889
And when you dig into a place,
things are always more complicated,

00:44:02.294 --> 00:44:05.179
nuanced, and I'd say interesting

00:44:05.940 --> 00:44:09.281
once you start tapping in,
because Memphis is one of those

00:44:09.348 --> 00:44:13.255
underdog places
that you go in thinking it's gonna

00:44:14.035 --> 00:44:15.378
not be like this.

00:44:15.445 --> 00:44:18.923
And then when you get into
these parts of the town, you're like, wow,

00:44:18.990 --> 00:44:20.872
it's really a pleasant surprise.

00:44:25.777 --> 00:44:28.391
-We watch your videos.
-You watch my videos?

00:44:28.458 --> 00:44:29.418
-Yeah.
-Thank you.

00:44:29.485 --> 00:44:31.401
I saw you and I was like, "Oh my God."

00:44:31.468 --> 00:44:33.545
-Yeah. I'm making a video on Memphis.

00:44:33.970 --> 00:44:36.201
-It's pretty cool.
-Yeah. It's a nice city.

00:44:36.268 --> 00:44:39.843
But with all the voting stuff
going on right now,

00:44:39.910 --> 00:44:43.737
-The gerrymandering, like they…
-Okay. They're doing that here?

00:44:43.804 --> 00:44:47.903
-Yeah. It's like they split up
because we're like the only Democratic…

00:44:47.970 --> 00:44:50.383
-…like congressional district.
-Sure, yeah.

00:44:50.450 --> 00:44:53.076
They just split it up.
So that's like a big…

00:44:53.143 --> 00:44:54.223
-I hate that.

00:44:54.290 --> 00:44:56.053
When I lived in California,

00:44:56.120 --> 00:44:59.393
what they did there, they took
this district in the far northeast,

00:44:59.460 --> 00:45:03.638
which is basically ranchers,
connect that all the way down

00:45:03.705 --> 00:45:06.647
to Marin County,
which is just north of San Francisco.

00:45:06.714 --> 00:45:08.413
Which is gonna vote Left.

00:45:08.480 --> 00:45:11.355
So it's not representative
of those people up there.

00:45:11.422 --> 00:45:14.286
And I just feel like
everyone's playing this dirty game.

00:45:14.353 --> 00:45:15.499
-Yeah.
-I hate it.

00:45:15.566 --> 00:45:17.893
I don't know how it's allowed in our system

00:45:17.960 --> 00:45:20.189
because it takes people's power away.

00:45:20.256 --> 00:45:23.058
Yeah. 
And especially in a state like Tennessee.

00:45:23.125 --> 00:45:25.606
-Where it's majority Republican, obviously.
-Yeah.

00:45:25.673 --> 00:45:28.881
But like Memphis and Nashville
are majority Democrat.

00:45:28.948 --> 00:45:32.123
And especially in a city like Memphis
where it's majority Black.

00:45:32.190 --> 00:45:33.106
Yeah.

00:45:33.173 --> 00:45:37.291
And like it's just underrepresented and
it was gonna be a big like congressional,

00:45:37.358 --> 00:45:39.533
I guess the elections this year, next year.

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:40.483
Okay. Yep.

00:45:40.550 --> 00:45:43.588
I mean, I'm only 17.
So I'll vote next year.

00:45:43.655 --> 00:45:45.566
-Yeah.
-But you don't want to see this.

00:45:45.633 --> 00:45:47.412
Yeah. This is bad for our city.

00:45:47.480 --> 00:45:51.825
People talk bad about the city,
like LeBron James, you just saw.

00:45:51.892 --> 00:45:54.429
-I didn't know. 
-He was talking bad about Memphis.

00:45:54.496 --> 00:45:57.052
-Why? Is he from here?
-No, he's not even from here.

00:45:57.119 --> 00:45:59.619
He's just saying
it's the worst place to come play.

00:45:59.686 --> 00:46:02.686
There's nothing to do,
which gave a sense of like city pride.

00:46:02.753 --> 00:46:06.263
Yeah. From the outside,
it's way cooler than I thought it would be.

00:46:06.836 --> 00:46:08.809
You live here, Tom. You're from Memphis?

00:46:08.876 --> 00:46:10.502
-I'm from…
-Okay. How is it?

00:46:10.569 --> 00:46:12.019
What's your take on the city?

00:46:12.086 --> 00:46:15.218
Well, it's a, it's a fun place to be
is all I can tell you.

00:46:15.285 --> 00:46:17.643
It's been good to me and I love it.

00:46:17.710 --> 00:46:18.588
You're a drummer?

00:46:18.655 --> 00:46:20.987
I'm a drummer and this town…

00:46:21.054 --> 00:46:23.760
Matter with your finger
that it's got that hair on?

00:46:23.827 --> 00:46:27.939
Yeah. I just I'm getting at that age
where like the ears get bigger and.

00:46:28.006 --> 00:46:31.004
-You need to see a doctor.
-Trim nose hair, you know.

00:46:31.071 --> 00:46:32.270
But this is…

00:46:32.337 --> 00:46:34.205
-It's serious. Yeah.
-It's serious.

00:46:34.272 --> 00:46:36.421
Okay. So look, yes, I'll be serious here.

00:46:36.488 --> 00:46:38.664
-No, don't.
-I won't be able to do it.

00:46:39.722 --> 00:46:44.152
Yes. I'm a musician. I play drums
and I've been playing in this town

00:46:44.219 --> 00:46:46.864
for 50 or 60 years.

00:46:46.931 --> 00:46:48.453
I'm not gonna say how many.

00:46:48.916 --> 00:46:51.830
And it's afforded me a fun lifestyle.
I'll put it that way.

00:46:51.897 --> 00:46:52.829
Jazz drummer?

00:46:52.896 --> 00:46:55.780
I'm a jazz drummer primarily,
but I play everything.

00:46:55.847 --> 00:46:58.051
I play country. I play in a brass band.

00:46:58.118 --> 00:47:00.858
I'm in a couple of jazz trios.

00:47:00.925 --> 00:47:02.253
I do recording.

00:47:02.320 --> 00:47:06.164
-What's your take on the city?
Memphis, let's just call a spade a spade.

00:47:06.231 --> 00:47:08.213
It has a bad reputation, unfortunately.

00:47:08.290 --> 00:47:09.546
Here's what it is, man.

00:47:09.613 --> 00:47:11.744
There's bad things everywhere
in the world.

00:47:11.811 --> 00:47:13.321
I don't care where you went.

00:47:13.388 --> 00:47:15.898
You're gonna find some negative business.

00:47:16.550 --> 00:47:20.780
You can either deal with that or you can
work around it and go your way.

00:47:20.847 --> 00:47:23.248
So I believe that's what you do in life.

00:47:23.315 --> 00:47:25.557
And therefore,
Memphis is a beautiful place.

00:47:25.624 --> 00:47:27.363
Great weather. Great people.

00:47:27.430 --> 00:47:29.325
Everybody's friendly. It's easy.

00:47:30.072 --> 00:47:31.679
It's not a hassle.

00:47:31.746 --> 00:47:33.373
We've got our issues and things.

00:47:33.440 --> 00:47:34.955
But, you know, we deal with,

00:47:35.336 --> 00:47:38.213
for instance, race.
Let's just talk about race.

00:47:38.280 --> 00:47:41.306
We deal with it
better than most people, most places.

00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:45.058
We've been dealing with it
a lot longer than most cities.

00:47:45.125 --> 00:47:49.573
And I play in bands that are integrated,
and it's wonderful.

00:47:49.640 --> 00:47:51.211
It's just absolutely wonderful.

00:47:51.278 --> 00:47:54.549
We all accept each other
and love each other

00:47:54.616 --> 00:47:56.487
just like anybody else.

00:47:56.554 --> 00:48:00.503
So all of that and, you know,
economics and all that.

00:48:00.570 --> 00:48:02.683
-Every city's got that, man.
-Yeah.

00:48:02.750 --> 00:48:04.961
And then Stella
is who hooked me up with you.

00:48:05.028 --> 00:48:07.128
-Stella the neighbor.
-Stella's a neighbor.

00:48:07.195 --> 00:48:09.383
And what a friendly, fun neighbor she is.

00:48:09.450 --> 00:48:12.403
She's got a great brain.
She's in the honor society.

00:48:12.470 --> 00:48:16.420
She's gonna be graduating next year
and going to, what's it, Yale?

00:48:17.183 --> 00:48:19.176
-Maybe if I'm lucky.
-If she's lucky.

00:48:19.625 --> 00:48:23.485
Some college where she will further
her education and learn that

00:48:23.552 --> 00:48:28.199
she probably shouldn't have gone to college
anyway, but that's something you do.

00:48:28.962 --> 00:48:30.543
Because when you get out…

00:48:30.610 --> 00:48:33.375
You've got to learn how to do
whatever that was.

00:48:34.240 --> 00:48:35.814
But it's gonna be great.

00:48:36.223 --> 00:48:38.336
And Chris has been the best neighbor ever.

00:48:38.403 --> 00:48:40.297
-That's Stella's father.
-Oh.

00:48:40.760 --> 00:48:44.018
Chris and I sit on the porch
and chew the fat.

00:48:44.085 --> 00:48:45.025
-Do you?
-Yeah.

00:48:45.092 --> 00:48:46.644
He's a fun man to chew it with.

00:48:46.711 --> 00:48:51.833
During COVID, Tom would put on
porch concerts for the whole neighborhood.

00:48:51.900 --> 00:48:54.263
He is very generous with his art.

00:48:54.330 --> 00:48:56.872
We'd have people
setting up chairs out here.

00:48:56.939 --> 00:48:59.057
It was the first music I saw during COVID.

00:48:59.124 --> 00:49:00.881
It was like an oasis in the desert.

00:49:00.948 --> 00:49:02.898
Well, that's beautiful right there, man.

00:49:02.965 --> 00:49:04.311
Designed to heal you up.

00:49:04.803 --> 00:49:07.681
-You know what I learned today?
-What did you learn today?

00:49:07.748 --> 00:49:09.715
-Tell me your name again.
-Peter.

00:49:09.782 --> 00:49:12.251
-I don't know why I didn't put it together.
-What?

00:49:12.318 --> 00:49:16.721
Music out of Memphis is really a fusion
of the Delta, the blues of the Delta,

00:49:16.788 --> 00:49:19.120
the folk of the sticks of Tennessee.

00:49:19.687 --> 00:49:21.491
-And then gospel.
-Oh.

00:49:21.558 --> 00:49:24.040
-Smashing together.
-That's what makes it good.

00:49:24.107 --> 00:49:25.205
It's a confluence.

00:49:25.272 --> 00:49:28.476
-And then rock and roll came out of that.
-And look where we are.

00:49:28.543 --> 00:49:31.493
We're right in between Mississippi,
Arkansas, and Tennessee.

00:49:31.560 --> 00:49:35.706
So all these rural farms that used to be
plantations and whatever,

00:49:35.773 --> 00:49:39.924
all these people would come to Memphis on
the weekends and hang out on Beale Street

00:49:39.991 --> 00:49:43.314
and bring their songs
and their stories and their business

00:49:43.381 --> 00:49:45.706
and swap ideas and make it happen.

00:49:45.773 --> 00:49:47.073
And that's still going on.

00:49:47.140 --> 00:49:51.716
Maybe it's not quite like that now,
but the interaction is amazing.

00:49:51.783 --> 00:49:54.646
-Your music scene is still happening here?
-Oh, yeah.

00:49:54.713 --> 00:49:56.530
-A lot of live music?
-Uh-huh.

00:49:56.597 --> 00:50:00.596
That's probably 25 venues a night
in the city that plays music.

00:50:00.663 --> 00:50:01.650
-Are you serious?

00:50:01.717 --> 00:50:04.967
Maybe not on Monday and Tuesday nights,
but on other nights, yeah.

00:50:05.034 --> 00:50:07.890
-You've made your life as a musician here?
-Isn't that incredible?

00:50:07.970 --> 00:50:10.420
And can you live in
a nice neighborhood like this?

00:50:10.487 --> 00:50:13.372
Yeah, but now I was smart with the money.

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:18.055
You're doing my dream.
I always wanted to play the drums.

00:50:18.122 --> 00:50:19.944
Why wouldn't you just do it, Peter?

00:50:20.011 --> 00:50:22.395
Yeah, 'cause I'm doing this,
I'm talking to you.

00:50:22.462 --> 00:50:24.813
Look here. Life is that long. Okay?

00:50:24.895 --> 00:50:27.130
Go get a drum set.
Everybody needs one.

00:50:27.660 --> 00:50:28.839
Hey.

00:50:28.906 --> 00:50:30.751
-How you doing?
-[gasps] Oh my God.

00:50:30.818 --> 00:50:32.986
How you doing?
Are you the mother of Stella?

00:50:33.053 --> 00:50:34.943
This is Scarlett, mother of Stella.

00:50:35.010 --> 00:50:37.010
You're doing a great job. And your name?

00:50:37.077 --> 00:50:38.953
-Chris. Good to meet you.
-Chris.

00:50:39.020 --> 00:50:40.422
Stella made all this happen.

00:50:40.489 --> 00:50:42.888
I was walking by, talking into the camera,

00:50:42.955 --> 00:50:46.273
and she was the only one out and about
and she was very friendly.

00:50:46.340 --> 00:50:48.309
What are your thoughts on the city?

00:50:48.376 --> 00:50:52.653
It's a city with, like,
the greatest soul in the world.

00:50:52.720 --> 00:50:55.969
We have our problems, for sure.

00:50:56.036 --> 00:51:01.384
And there's a ton of inequity
and all of that.

00:51:01.940 --> 00:51:04.410
But we've lived here…

00:51:04.477 --> 00:51:08.432
I moved here in 2001.

00:51:08.499 --> 00:51:09.382
-Okay.

00:51:09.449 --> 00:51:12.333
I haven't ever experienced violent crime.

00:51:12.400 --> 00:51:14.206
I don't live in fear.

00:51:14.273 --> 00:51:16.908
Like, the media will

00:51:17.504 --> 00:51:20.865
make you feel like
it's the scariest place in the world.

00:51:20.932 --> 00:51:23.604
-Right.
They sort of fester on Memphis, don't they?

00:51:23.671 --> 00:51:26.632
I raised my children
right here in the middle.

00:51:26.699 --> 00:51:29.602
They run the streets and ride their bikes

00:51:29.669 --> 00:51:32.308
and do all the things.

00:51:32.375 --> 00:51:35.543
And we don't live in fear here.

00:51:35.610 --> 00:51:38.033
It's just such a misconception.

00:51:38.100 --> 00:51:40.560
-Little Rock, another one of those cities.

00:51:40.627 --> 00:51:43.415
I was actually surprised.
It was nicer than I thought.

00:51:43.482 --> 00:51:44.384
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:51:44.451 --> 00:51:47.516
And all of these places,
it depends where you are in the city.

00:51:47.583 --> 00:51:50.702
-That's right.
-And I totally recognize that.

00:51:50.769 --> 00:51:53.313
Like, we're, you know, very blessed.

00:51:53.380 --> 00:51:56.707
But it's just a cool, cool city.
Yeah.

00:51:57.450 --> 00:52:00.162
-Thank you, guys.
-My little daughter making it happen.

00:52:00.229 --> 00:52:02.126
-Stella, you made it happen.

00:52:02.193 --> 00:52:04.653
Thank you. You're welcome to Memphis.

00:52:04.720 --> 00:52:07.666
Tom, I just hope you continue to misbehave.

00:52:08.309 --> 00:52:09.992
What else is there to do?

00:52:10.059 --> 00:52:10.942
[laughter]

00:52:15.685 --> 00:52:17.823
[Bill] This is another cool Memphis area.

00:52:17.890 --> 00:52:19.444
It's called Overton Park.

00:52:19.511 --> 00:52:21.223
-It just keeps going?
-Yeah.

00:52:21.290 --> 00:52:24.066
-Bill, this is…
-Another lovely midtown neighborhood.

00:52:24.133 --> 00:52:26.776
What's interesting about this neighborhood

00:52:26.843 --> 00:52:31.653
is John Overton was
a well-known Memphis conservationist.

00:52:31.720 --> 00:52:34.206
He created Overton Park.

00:52:34.273 --> 00:52:36.449
Which has now been given to the city.

00:52:36.894 --> 00:52:42.340
And it's a municipal golf course
and a big open green space area

00:52:42.407 --> 00:52:45.963
and an untouched forest
right in the middle of the city

00:52:46.030 --> 00:52:48.451
that butts up to the Memphis Zoo.

00:52:48.518 --> 00:52:50.442
And why I'm saying this is

00:52:50.509 --> 00:52:54.033
another thing that people
just don't know about Memphis is

00:52:54.100 --> 00:52:57.231
our zoo has been ranked
the number one zoo in the country

00:52:57.298 --> 00:52:59.050
many years, different years.

00:52:59.117 --> 00:53:01.292
Who would think that in Memphis, right?

00:53:01.359 --> 00:53:04.493
-I wouldn't think any of this
in Memphis, to be honest.

00:53:04.560 --> 00:53:06.653
-Well, there's a lot to our city.

00:53:06.720 --> 00:53:11.560
This massive Gothic building
was the Sears Tower in Memphis.

00:53:12.055 --> 00:53:14.358
That was really the precursor to Amazon.

00:53:14.425 --> 00:53:17.384
Back when Sears and Roebuck had catalogs,

00:53:17.451 --> 00:53:19.862
floors and floors and floors of people

00:53:19.929 --> 00:53:22.286
taking all the catalog orders
and shipping them.

00:53:22.353 --> 00:53:26.353
And this building, the perspective
that you're getting right now…

00:53:26.420 --> 00:53:29.025
-See how far back it goes?
-Oh, yeah. Way back there.

00:53:29.092 --> 00:53:30.163
It's massive.

00:53:30.230 --> 00:53:32.414
-So, you're talking nationwide?

00:53:32.481 --> 00:53:34.422
Nationwide. This was the catalog…

00:53:34.820 --> 00:53:38.742
So, how did it work? People would actually
send a letter with what they wanted?

00:53:38.809 --> 00:53:40.735
-Did you ever see a Sears catalog?
-No.

00:53:40.802 --> 00:53:43.779
Okay, so a Sears catalog
was just like a phone book,

00:53:43.846 --> 00:53:45.888
except it was glossy paper.

00:53:45.955 --> 00:53:49.678
If you ever went in a Sears, you could buy
anything from a craftsman wrench

00:53:49.745 --> 00:53:52.433
to a pair of underwear,
to a vacuum cleaner,

00:53:52.500 --> 00:53:55.773
to an icebox,
to a pair of women's shoes in a Sears.

00:53:55.840 --> 00:53:56.723
-Hmm.

00:53:56.790 --> 00:54:00.077
The catalog had every single item
they ever carried in it.

00:54:00.513 --> 00:54:03.573
And if you lived off
in a rural area somewhere,

00:54:03.640 --> 00:54:07.667
everybody had a Sears catalog,
and you would call Sears up and order it.

00:54:08.140 --> 00:54:12.171
So, you had floors and floors and floors
of people answering the phone,

00:54:12.238 --> 00:54:16.248
taking orders, processing the orders,
and getting it shipped to you.

00:54:16.835 --> 00:54:19.986
About 20 years ago…
look how big the son of a b*tch is.

00:54:20.053 --> 00:54:22.511
-Look. It's huge. 
-It just keeps going. Yeah.

00:54:22.578 --> 00:54:24.373
About 15, 20 years ago,

00:54:24.440 --> 00:54:27.082
when Amazon took over
that kind of business,

00:54:27.430 --> 00:54:30.199
Sears shut down
the whole Sears catalog thing.

00:54:30.723 --> 00:54:32.904
And this place was shut down, boarded up,

00:54:32.971 --> 00:54:35.961
and there was not
a single human being in it for 20 years.

00:54:36.029 --> 00:54:38.921
And here we are in Midtown, Memphis

00:54:38.988 --> 00:54:41.306
with this massive property here.

00:54:41.373 --> 00:54:43.852
As you can imagine, just like Chicago,

00:54:43.919 --> 00:54:47.256
all of the neighborhood
around here was blighted.

00:54:47.323 --> 00:54:48.623
-Everyone was working here.

00:54:48.690 --> 00:54:50.513
Because all the business went away.

00:54:50.580 --> 00:54:52.899
Some developers got together
and repurposed it.

00:54:52.966 --> 00:54:54.865
So, now this building has in it,

00:54:55.872 --> 00:54:58.578
office buildings,
it's got a high school in it.

00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:01.288
It's got restaurants in it.

00:55:01.355 --> 00:55:02.726
It's got everything in it.

00:55:02.793 --> 00:55:06.033
And it has the largest

00:55:06.100 --> 00:55:08.816
not-for-profit doctor's office

00:55:09.556 --> 00:55:11.045
called Church Health Center

00:55:11.474 --> 00:55:14.173
that is specifically for the working poor.

00:55:14.240 --> 00:55:16.056
You, inside this place,

00:55:16.123 --> 00:55:18.603
in the Church Health Center
on the backside,

00:55:18.670 --> 00:55:21.079
can get world-class primary care.

00:55:21.947 --> 00:55:23.114
-Free?
-Free.

00:55:23.181 --> 00:55:24.107
-Or cheap?
-Free.

00:55:27.790 --> 00:55:30.802
[Peter] Love it when things are done
in just such cool styles.

00:55:30.869 --> 00:55:33.893
Something as boring as
a parking garage looks cool.

00:55:35.083 --> 00:55:37.790
-How are you ladies doing?
-You gonna take our picture?

00:55:37.857 --> 00:55:40.739
I'm doing a video on Memphis.
Can I ask you a question?

00:55:40.806 --> 00:55:42.277
-Sure.
-How is this place?

00:55:42.720 --> 00:55:43.779
-Crosstown?
-Yeah.

00:55:43.846 --> 00:55:45.276
-It's awesome.
-It's awesome?

00:55:45.343 --> 00:55:46.533
Definitely look inside.

00:55:46.600 --> 00:55:49.052
And do all ladies
style like you here in Memphis?

00:55:49.119 --> 00:55:50.485
[laughter]

00:55:50.552 --> 00:55:51.435
You're styling.

00:55:52.216 --> 00:55:54.073
-How's the city?
-It's great.

00:55:54.544 --> 00:55:55.562
-You love it?

00:55:55.629 --> 00:55:59.138
Don't listen to the media
when they say, you know…

00:55:59.205 --> 00:56:00.987
-Memphis is great.
-Okay.

00:56:01.054 --> 00:56:03.424
-It's firing on all cylinders right now?
-Mm-hmm.

00:56:03.491 --> 00:56:04.856
Good music, food, culture?

00:56:04.923 --> 00:56:06.276
-Oh, definitely.
-Of course.

00:56:06.343 --> 00:56:07.549
Okay, cool.

00:56:07.616 --> 00:56:09.284
Barbecue. Get some barbecue.

00:56:09.351 --> 00:56:11.326
-Better than Texas?
-[scoffs]

00:56:11.393 --> 00:56:14.132
-[Bill] Because it is.  Everybody knows.
-Hands down.

00:56:14.199 --> 00:56:15.262
And here's the thing.

00:56:15.329 --> 00:56:18.077
In Texas, they make brisket,
which is beef.

00:56:18.665 --> 00:56:21.777
Don't you know you're supposed to
grill beef and barbecue pork?

00:56:21.844 --> 00:56:23.717
-They got it all backwards.
-[giggling]

00:56:23.784 --> 00:56:25.171
-See what I'm saying?
-Mm-hmm.

00:56:25.238 --> 00:56:27.953
-There's a science behind it.
-There is a science behind it.

00:56:28.396 --> 00:56:31.596
Bill's on his phone, which means
he's putting something together.

00:56:31.663 --> 00:56:34.263
I'm getting in touch with the guy
who runs this show.

00:56:34.330 --> 00:56:35.312
-No way. Nice.

00:56:35.379 --> 00:56:36.972
-Check it out.
-Wow!

00:56:37.928 --> 00:56:39.695
-Look at that.
-Yeah.

00:56:40.163 --> 00:56:42.774
They purposely left
a lot of the original stuff

00:56:42.841 --> 00:56:44.861
as an homage to what it once was.

00:56:44.928 --> 00:56:48.510
This is the graduating class
of the high school that's in here,

00:56:48.577 --> 00:56:50.309
Crosstown High School.

00:56:50.376 --> 00:56:52.689
-This is Todd.
-Todd. Nice to meet you.

00:56:53.449 --> 00:56:56.183
-I guess you manage this place.
-I do.

00:56:56.250 --> 00:56:58.950
-And he's one of the co-founders.
-For better or worse.

00:56:59.017 --> 00:57:02.507
So he was one of the guys
that took this building

00:57:02.574 --> 00:57:05.593
from an abandoned Sears and made it this.

00:57:05.660 --> 00:57:09.323
[Todd] Built in 1927,
1.5 million square feet.

00:57:09.390 --> 00:57:12.558
-Wow.
-So 25 football fields of space.

00:57:13.105 --> 00:57:16.694
The building's so long that if you
were to turn it kind of on its head,

00:57:16.761 --> 00:57:18.440
that that ends at the top.

00:57:18.507 --> 00:57:21.633
It'd be 200 feet taller
than the Chrysler Building in New York.

00:57:21.700 --> 00:57:24.873
The way it works is
it's basically a vertical city.

00:57:24.940 --> 00:57:27.715
The ground floor is an old town square.

00:57:27.782 --> 00:57:28.665
Yep.

00:57:28.732 --> 00:57:33.958
You've got six restaurants, grocery,
coffee obviously, ice cream, pharmacy,

00:57:34.025 --> 00:57:36.523
hair salon, nail salon, bank branch, etc.

00:57:36.590 --> 00:57:38.453
We have 72 tenants total,

00:57:38.520 --> 00:57:42.196
half of which are nonprofits
doing important work around Memphis.

00:57:42.263 --> 00:57:44.200
But arts and music is the spark.

00:57:44.267 --> 00:57:46.241
It's the lifeblood of the building.

00:57:46.308 --> 00:57:49.308
The owner of the building
had a vision back in 2010.

00:57:49.729 --> 00:57:53.796
It had been empty for over 20 years
by the time we got started.

00:57:53.863 --> 00:57:56.472
And so it was just a daunting task
with so much…

00:57:56.539 --> 00:57:59.731
-I can't imagine.
-…space, and it was the recession.

00:57:59.798 --> 00:58:03.843
So who's investing this much money?
Believes this much in the city?

00:58:03.910 --> 00:58:06.541
-Because this is a huge reno.
-It's massive, yeah.

00:58:06.608 --> 00:58:09.823
Over 30 different sources of funding
came together.

00:58:09.890 --> 00:58:11.248
-Okay. Mostly local?

00:58:11.730 --> 00:58:13.160
-So mostly local.

00:58:13.227 --> 00:58:15.323
We used new market tax credits,

00:58:15.390 --> 00:58:19.263
federal incentives to encourage
development in low-income census tracts.

00:58:19.330 --> 00:58:22.346
And then historic tax credits
that are federal incentives

00:58:22.413 --> 00:58:24.661
to incentivize rehabbing old buildings.

00:58:24.728 --> 00:58:26.503
-Are both parties pushing those?

00:58:26.570 --> 00:58:28.373
-Seems like it. Yeah.
-Okay.

00:58:28.440 --> 00:58:29.630
It seems like it.

00:58:30.090 --> 00:58:30.973
How you doing?

00:58:31.040 --> 00:58:33.685
-This is the Memphis Listening Lab.
-Oh, nice.

00:58:33.752 --> 00:58:37.468
So I always say if Crosstown's a
vertical city, this is our public library.

00:58:37.535 --> 00:58:40.663
But because it's Memphis,
it's music instead of books.

00:58:40.730 --> 00:58:45.027
So a guy named John King,
he would have been 83 I think this year,

00:58:45.094 --> 00:58:46.693
passed away a few years ago.

00:58:46.760 --> 00:58:50.413
-But he collected, 50,000 albums.
-Wow.

00:58:50.480 --> 00:58:53.881
So you can figure out what
you're interested in, tell the folks here,

00:58:53.948 --> 00:58:57.548
they'll pull the album, and then
you come back to the listening stations.

00:58:57.615 --> 00:58:58.763
-Oh, nice.

00:58:58.830 --> 00:59:01.998
-You just look out on the city and chill.
-Chill.

00:59:02.065 --> 00:59:05.483
There's high school classes,
music appreciation classes in here.

00:59:06.562 --> 00:59:08.343
-This is cool. I've never seen this.

00:59:08.410 --> 00:59:09.713
Yeah. It's pretty amazing.

00:59:09.780 --> 00:59:11.853
[Bill] It's a library for vinyl.

00:59:11.920 --> 00:59:14.266
-You guys from here?
-I'm from Mississippi.

00:59:14.333 --> 00:59:16.690
I'm from St. Louis,
but I grew up mostly here.

00:59:16.757 --> 00:59:18.743
-Okay. What city do you like better?

00:59:18.810 --> 00:59:20.985
Oh, I would say I like Memphis better.

00:59:21.052 --> 00:59:21.935
-Okay.

00:59:23.675 --> 00:59:25.233
-What about you?

00:59:25.300 --> 00:59:26.596
Mississippi, man.

00:59:26.663 --> 00:59:30.436
I'm a hometown man, but Memphis
does have some great things here.

00:59:30.503 --> 00:59:32.537
-Okay. How's it working at this place?

00:59:32.604 --> 00:59:34.673
It's amazing. It's like a dream come true.

00:59:34.740 --> 00:59:36.449
-You love vinyl?

00:59:36.516 --> 00:59:38.142
-Oh, music major.
-Yeah.

00:59:38.209 --> 00:59:40.337
So probably one of the best places to be.

00:59:40.404 --> 00:59:42.704
-Yeah. This is my heaven.
-This is your heaven.

00:59:42.771 --> 00:59:45.897
Someone comes in here,
like, I want whatever record.

00:59:45.964 --> 00:59:48.199
-You're going up on the ladder? 
-Yeah.

00:59:49.313 --> 00:59:50.548
That is so cool, man.

00:59:52.770 --> 00:59:55.688
Behind that door is Southern Grooves
Recording Studio,

00:59:55.755 --> 00:59:58.590
and down the hall
is the headquarters of WYXR,

00:59:58.657 --> 01:00:01.483
so we have our own radio station as well.

01:00:01.550 --> 01:00:02.433
Okay, nice.

01:00:02.500 --> 01:00:04.766
So you've got broadcast,

01:00:04.833 --> 01:00:07.536
you've got Memphis Listening Lab education,

01:00:07.603 --> 01:00:09.881
you've got a recording studio next door.

01:00:09.948 --> 01:00:12.595
Crosstown Arts has two performance venues,

01:00:12.662 --> 01:00:17.213
so you've got performance, recording,
production, education, broadcast.

01:00:17.280 --> 01:00:21.182
All the people involved in the music-making
process all along the way,

01:00:21.249 --> 01:00:22.229
and they come here.

01:00:22.296 --> 01:00:24.380
-Is there stuff on every level?

01:00:24.447 --> 01:00:25.464
-Yes, there are.

01:00:25.531 --> 01:00:28.223
So, ground level
is all the restaurant retail.

01:00:28.290 --> 01:00:32.237
Two through six are church help,
Crosstown Arts, the high school,

01:00:32.304 --> 01:00:35.363
city leadership,
the 72 tenants that I mentioned.

01:00:35.813 --> 01:00:38.561
Floor 7 through 10 are 265 apartments.

01:00:38.628 --> 01:00:39.780
People are living here?

01:00:39.847 --> 01:00:41.775
So, my family lives on the 10th floor,

01:00:41.842 --> 01:00:45.024
my daughter goes to school on the 4th,
my office is on the 2nd.

01:00:45.091 --> 01:00:47.234
-You're serious?
-I do not have a car.

01:00:48.214 --> 01:00:50.483
-Your coffee shop's downstairs.
-That's right.

01:00:52.680 --> 01:00:55.553
This is Crosstown Arts,
the contemporary arts center.

01:00:55.620 --> 01:01:00.985
They have the performance venues,
gallery spaces, artist residency program,

01:01:01.052 --> 01:01:02.188
and the maker space.

01:01:02.260 --> 01:01:06.121
So, this is one of the music venues
called the Green Room.

01:01:06.188 --> 01:01:08.073
It's a small music lounge.

01:01:08.140 --> 01:01:09.151
-Hey, Alex.
-Hey, Todd.

01:01:09.218 --> 01:01:11.025
-How are you?
-I'm good. How are you?

01:01:11.092 --> 01:01:13.892
So, these guys, it's live music
three, four times a week.

01:01:13.959 --> 01:01:14.835
-Oh, awesome.

01:01:14.902 --> 01:01:19.615
-Everything from singer-songwriter,
to amazing jazz and blues,

01:01:19.682 --> 01:01:21.513
and hip-hop and DJ,

01:01:21.580 --> 01:01:22.797
all the genres.

01:01:22.864 --> 01:01:25.513
I got to take you in here, Peter.
It's called Art Bar.

01:01:25.580 --> 01:01:28.428
It's called Art Bar
because the top of the bar lifts up

01:01:28.495 --> 01:01:32.458
and you install art pieces
that are then exhibited by artists.

01:01:32.525 --> 01:01:35.523
And then this great cocktail menu.

01:01:35.590 --> 01:01:39.531
We're now in the tower of the building
and it just keeps going back.

01:01:40.977 --> 01:01:43.495
-So, nighttime this comes alive?
-Yeah, absolutely.

01:01:43.562 --> 01:01:47.909
So, this is 5:00 to midnight
every day of the week except for Monday.

01:01:48.430 --> 01:01:51.998
-So, not long ago, someone's in Mississippi

01:01:52.472 --> 01:01:54.854
ordering some socks and a candle.

01:01:54.921 --> 01:01:55.804
Right.

01:01:55.871 --> 01:01:58.483
And someone's
putting that order together right here.

01:01:58.550 --> 01:02:01.379
This neighborhood is flourishing
because of all the jobs.

01:02:01.446 --> 01:02:03.799
Sears goes away, neighborhood goes down.

01:02:04.210 --> 01:02:05.723
You guys have come in.

01:02:05.790 --> 01:02:08.246
You're bringing back
something into the community.

01:02:08.313 --> 01:02:11.242
Not gonna have the manufacturing jobs
as before, obviously.

01:02:11.309 --> 01:02:12.192
-Right.

01:02:12.259 --> 01:02:14.921
Well, when Sears was here,
there were 1,500 employees.

01:02:14.988 --> 01:02:17.253
And funny enough,
there's 1,500 employees now.

01:02:17.320 --> 01:02:19.822
-Seriously?
-In the 72 organizations.

01:02:19.888 --> 01:02:21.565
-Oh, that's awesome.

01:02:21.632 --> 01:02:23.463
-So, for 20 years, there was nothing.

01:02:23.530 --> 01:02:26.436
And now, between the residents,
employees, students,

01:02:26.503 --> 01:02:27.957
just people coming and going,

01:02:28.024 --> 01:02:30.583
there's 3,000 people
coming and going every day.

01:02:30.650 --> 01:02:33.023
-So, that must have helped
the neighborhood out a bit.

01:02:33.090 --> 01:02:34.615
-Massively.
-Okay.

01:02:34.682 --> 01:02:35.565
-Big time.
-Yeah.

01:02:36.600 --> 01:02:39.800
-[Bill] This is how I remember it.
-So, y'all came in this front.

01:02:39.867 --> 01:02:42.489
When we got started,
that's what it looked like.

01:02:42.556 --> 01:02:45.234
-This is where we walked in this place.
-Right.

01:02:45.301 --> 01:02:46.463
Wow.

01:02:46.530 --> 01:02:48.656
-How much was this project, Todd?

01:02:48.723 --> 01:02:50.601
About $220 million.

01:02:51.205 --> 01:02:54.564
This is how it used to be,
orders would come in up on the 2nd floor.

01:02:54.631 --> 01:02:57.825
They'd put them in
these pneumatic tubes to the upper floors.

01:02:57.892 --> 01:03:00.942
They'd grab the goods,
put them down these huge spiral chutes

01:03:01.009 --> 01:03:04.028
that went all 10 floors,
came down to a conveyor belt

01:03:04.095 --> 01:03:07.300
that a the truck on the south side,
train on the north side.

01:03:07.367 --> 01:03:10.917
This is the last day of the retail store
before it went out of business.

01:03:10.984 --> 01:03:14.229
This is what it was in 2010
when we got started and where we are.

01:03:14.296 --> 01:03:15.699
This is 1927.

01:03:15.766 --> 01:03:21.399
Crazy fact, the original structure,
620,000 square feet in 1927.

01:03:21.466 --> 01:03:23.346
They built that in 180 days.

01:03:23.413 --> 01:03:24.296
Six months.

01:03:26.218 --> 01:03:29.594
So, all these atria that you're seeing,
those were just floors.

01:03:29.661 --> 01:03:32.245
-We cut all that out. We saw cut…
-Oh, no way.

01:03:32.312 --> 01:03:33.876
-Yeah, so we…
-That was a floor?

01:03:33.943 --> 01:03:37.155
Those were all floors.
We saw cut through three miles of concrete

01:03:37.222 --> 01:03:41.738
and took out 52 million pounds
of concrete to create the atrium.

01:03:42.320 --> 01:03:45.614
-All right. Guys…
-Come on out to the 10th floor balcony.

01:03:47.085 --> 01:03:48.405
She's having a good time.

01:03:48.472 --> 01:03:50.279
So, is that my daughter?

01:03:50.346 --> 01:03:52.493
-Do you know her, Todd?
-I might know her.

01:03:52.560 --> 01:03:54.608
Her name is Corinne Richardson.

01:03:54.675 --> 01:03:57.513
She's 16, 10th grader at Crosstown High.

01:03:57.580 --> 01:03:59.631
And she's just chilling out here.

01:03:59.698 --> 01:04:02.162
-She's at Crosstown?
-She's not in school right now.

01:04:02.229 --> 01:04:04.515
Let's see what time it is.
Oh, it's 3:47, so…

01:04:04.582 --> 01:04:05.689
…AP testing.

01:04:05.756 --> 01:04:07.055
-You what?
-AP testing.

01:04:07.122 --> 01:04:08.786
AP testing. Okay.

01:04:08.853 --> 01:04:11.343
I'm giving a little tour.
We're doing some video.

01:04:11.410 --> 01:04:14.243
Say hi to Peter.
He only has 4.2 million followers.

01:04:14.310 --> 01:04:15.937
Hi. Nice to meet you.

01:04:16.004 --> 01:04:18.507
-How is it going to high school here?
-Awesome.

01:04:18.574 --> 01:04:20.124
-You're loving it?
-It's so fun.

01:04:20.191 --> 01:04:23.592
-You just go down the building?
-And I'm late every single day.

01:04:23.660 --> 01:04:26.688
That's true. She takes the stairs
to high school every day.

01:04:26.755 --> 01:04:27.658
-That's not bad.

01:04:27.725 --> 01:04:30.203
-Literally wake up 5 minutes before class.
-I do.

01:04:30.270 --> 01:04:33.290
Is your dad winning
the cool dad award of the year?

01:04:33.357 --> 01:04:34.908
100% Yeah.

01:04:34.975 --> 01:04:37.722
-Right answer. Right answer.
Ding, ding, ding.

01:04:37.789 --> 01:04:39.646
-Killing it in this environment.

01:04:39.734 --> 01:04:41.310
Oh, yeah. It's so fun.

01:04:42.280 --> 01:04:44.811
Todd's 16-year-old daughter
still really likes him,

01:04:44.878 --> 01:04:46.681
so that's an amazing accomplishment.

01:04:46.748 --> 01:04:49.414
Yeah. Well, we still got
a little ways to go.

01:04:49.481 --> 01:04:51.683
-[Bill] That's quite uncommon.
-Uh-huh.

01:04:51.750 --> 01:04:54.037
So, are these rentals
or you're selling them?

01:04:54.104 --> 01:04:56.093
-They're rentals.
-What's the rent like?

01:04:56.160 --> 01:05:01.723
Market rate would be for a two-bedroom,
1,200 square feet, around $2,000.

01:05:01.790 --> 01:05:02.673
Okay.

01:05:02.740 --> 01:05:05.651
The other cool thing is
Memphis Teacher Residency.

01:05:05.718 --> 01:05:08.704
They recruit recent graduates to Memphis.

01:05:08.771 --> 01:05:11.878
They earn a tuition-free
master's in education.

01:05:11.945 --> 01:05:15.250
in return for teaching in Memphis'
highest-need urban schools

01:05:15.317 --> 01:05:16.820
for three years after that.

01:05:16.887 --> 01:05:20.243
And so, their training is here,
but they also live in the building.

01:05:20.310 --> 01:05:25.628
St. Jude rents 40 apartments
for their PhD visiting researchers.

01:05:25.695 --> 01:05:29.533
And then Crosstown Arts has 12 apartments
for their artist residency.

01:05:29.600 --> 01:05:33.280
So, of the 265, about 90 of them
are artists-in-residence,

01:05:33.347 --> 01:05:35.933
teachers-in-residence,
and doctors-in-residence.

01:05:36.390 --> 01:05:39.859
This is just a normal
one-bedroom apartment.

01:05:45.100 --> 01:05:47.135
-You got to love these pillars here.
-Yeah.

01:05:47.202 --> 01:05:49.437
And the ceiling height, it just…

01:05:50.160 --> 01:05:51.720
It's really great.

01:05:53.830 --> 01:05:56.316
-So, this would be
roughly two grand a month?

01:05:56.383 --> 01:05:57.954
Um, about. Yeah.

01:05:59.570 --> 01:06:01.489
-Oh, and you got a full balcony.

01:06:01.556 --> 01:06:02.846
-Yeah. 10th floor.

01:06:02.913 --> 01:06:03.854
-This is awesome.

01:06:07.500 --> 01:06:09.444
Hey, good sir. How's it going?

01:06:09.511 --> 01:06:10.592
-Question.
-Yeah.

01:06:10.659 --> 01:06:12.336
Y'all covering the event tonight?

01:06:12.403 --> 01:06:14.090
What y'all doing? Or organized?

01:06:14.157 --> 01:06:18.243
These guys… Todd knows it,
but go in and talk to whoever's in there.

01:06:18.310 --> 01:06:20.718
I don't know.
I'm just making a video on Memphis.

01:06:20.785 --> 01:06:23.669
-You're an officer?
-Mm-hmm. Crime scene investigator.

01:06:23.736 --> 01:06:24.619
How's it going?

01:06:25.127 --> 01:06:26.332
It's Memphis.

01:06:26.399 --> 01:06:27.614
-Which means?

01:06:27.681 --> 01:06:31.252
If you're doing something on Memphis,
you want to know about the crime?

01:06:31.319 --> 01:06:32.886
-I'm just…
-Local news.

01:06:32.953 --> 01:06:34.173
Watch the local news?

01:06:34.240 --> 01:06:35.759
It is exactly that.

01:06:36.286 --> 01:06:38.656
Memphis has been in
the top five for a reason.

01:06:38.723 --> 01:06:41.376
-But in a neighborhood like this
it feels pretty good.

01:06:41.443 --> 01:06:44.328
Well, I mean, Midtown is an eclectic area.

01:06:44.395 --> 01:06:47.851
So you've got
your good and your bad mixed together,

01:06:47.918 --> 01:06:51.617
and artistic, funky kind of juxtaposition

01:06:51.684 --> 01:06:54.713
with the location of the city
for what it is.

01:06:54.780 --> 01:06:57.851
-But police officers
always see a different side of the city.

01:06:57.918 --> 01:07:00.407
-Always.
-You guys are seeing the worst of it.

01:07:00.474 --> 01:07:02.249
We're seeing people at their worst.

01:07:02.316 --> 01:07:05.161
But crime's gone down a bit.
Homicides have gone down.

01:07:05.228 --> 01:07:08.406
You can say that by the numbers,
but the numbers are still high.

01:07:08.473 --> 01:07:11.815
Of the top ten,
Memphis is always in the top three.

01:07:12.400 --> 01:07:13.283
Always.

01:07:15.145 --> 01:07:16.763
-Thank you for your work, sir.

01:07:16.830 --> 01:07:19.890
Without you guys
everything falls apart in two seconds.

01:07:19.957 --> 01:07:22.545
Yeah. It probably would.
It would be lawless.

01:07:24.027 --> 01:07:24.910
-Take care.

01:07:32.260 --> 01:07:33.339
[Todd] Sorry, guys.

01:07:33.925 --> 01:07:35.483
Excuse us. Sorry.

01:07:36.541 --> 01:07:39.692
-Photo bomb. I apologize.
-I'm making the picture way uglier.

01:07:39.759 --> 01:07:41.463
Yeah, yeah. You right.

01:07:41.530 --> 01:07:42.737
[laughter]

01:07:42.804 --> 01:07:45.260
[Bill] Lord knows
you don't want me in there.

01:07:45.752 --> 01:07:49.052
[Todd] So I mentioned Crosstown Arts
is a contemporary arts center.

01:07:49.119 --> 01:07:52.801
Basically what we do is try to provide
resources for artists and musicians

01:07:52.867 --> 01:07:55.987
in Memphis to make their work
and to showcase their work.

01:07:56.054 --> 01:08:00.055
So in galleries for visual arts
or music venues for music.

01:08:00.122 --> 01:08:02.620
And in here is what they call shared art.

01:08:03.027 --> 01:08:05.262
So it's a membership-based maker space.

01:08:05.750 --> 01:08:08.169
-You can pay a monthly membership fee.
-Oh, nice.

01:08:08.236 --> 01:08:11.654
You have access to screen printing,
large format printing,

01:08:11.721 --> 01:08:13.993
laser cutter, digital media lab.

01:08:14.060 --> 01:08:18.518
It has all the software for film
and music and photography editing.

01:08:18.585 --> 01:08:22.696
And then sculptors and furniture makers
come in and have access

01:08:22.763 --> 01:08:25.518
to this full professional wood shop.

01:08:25.585 --> 01:08:26.636
Oh, wow.

01:08:26.703 --> 01:08:31.249
The other thing I love is this glass leads
into the entrance into the high school.

01:08:31.316 --> 01:08:34.680
So when they come into school,
they see all this happening.

01:08:34.747 --> 01:08:37.831
-Do they have shop class at high school?
-They have their own.

01:08:37.898 --> 01:08:41.153
-They have their own version of this.
-Oh, wow.

01:08:43.720 --> 01:08:45.146
I like to call them 901.

01:08:45.213 --> 01:08:47.023
That's our area code.

01:08:47.090 --> 01:08:48.572
-What's up? Juan?

01:08:48.639 --> 01:08:50.593
-Meet Peter.
-Peter. Nice to meet you.

01:08:50.660 --> 01:08:53.160
You're running
different ethnic cuisines every day.

01:08:53.227 --> 01:08:54.841
Yeah. We've got a lot every day.

01:08:54.908 --> 01:08:58.500
But we do have from Mexico,
Venezuela, and Sudan.

01:08:58.567 --> 01:09:01.684
We've had people from just about
any country you can imagine.

01:09:01.751 --> 01:09:03.943
Burundi, Bhutan, Pakistan.

01:09:04.010 --> 01:09:05.710
You got Ukraine. You have Borscht?

01:09:05.777 --> 01:09:08.263
-We've had employees from Ukraine.
-Okay.

01:09:08.330 --> 01:09:12.273
We also had a St. Jude patient
from Ukraine.

01:09:13.126 --> 01:09:16.607
Came at 17, worked for us
while she went through chemotherapy.

01:09:16.674 --> 01:09:19.262
-Wow.
-Had her leg amputated after 19 rounds.

01:09:19.329 --> 01:09:20.505
But was cancer-free.

01:09:20.940 --> 01:09:23.371
Completed her stint
without going back to Europe.

01:09:23.438 --> 01:09:26.138
Got married
and brought the husband three months later.

01:09:26.205 --> 01:09:27.455
[Todd] No way!

01:09:29.290 --> 01:09:31.636
Remember what I was saying it's provided.

01:09:32.096 --> 01:09:35.633
Working or you have to work 10, 11, 12.
How many hours?

01:09:35.700 --> 01:09:37.427
10 hours a week. You have to work.

01:09:37.494 --> 01:09:40.543
They provide quality health care
to the working uninsured.

01:09:40.610 --> 01:09:43.848
This is where you would
come in to see a doctor or nurse.

01:09:43.915 --> 01:09:45.277
You get primary care here.

01:09:45.344 --> 01:09:48.713
If you need specialist care,
then they have partnerships.

01:09:48.780 --> 01:09:52.191
Over 1,000 doctors and nurses
volunteer their time

01:09:52.258 --> 01:09:55.143
with Church Help
to provide the care that the people need.

01:09:55.210 --> 01:09:57.028
Okay. So I work 10 hours.

01:09:57.095 --> 01:09:59.925
I need health care.
I'm getting it here no matter what?

01:09:59.992 --> 01:10:02.266
You can come here. There's walk-in clinic.

01:10:02.333 --> 01:10:05.719
And then you can also come here
and participate in their…

01:10:05.786 --> 01:10:08.941
it's not insurance,
but it's their wellness care program.

01:10:09.008 --> 01:10:12.339
Depending on demand, right,
there may be a little time to wait,

01:10:12.406 --> 01:10:13.554
but you can get care.

01:10:13.621 --> 01:10:15.773
-If I need an MRI…
-Yep.

01:10:15.840 --> 01:10:18.667
So they will work with
Methodist University Hospital

01:10:18.734 --> 01:10:22.723
or Baptist Hospital,
the two main hospital systems in Memphis

01:10:22.790 --> 01:10:24.153
to get you what you need.

01:10:24.220 --> 01:10:27.346
Scott's both a Methodist minister
and a doctor.

01:10:27.413 --> 01:10:31.288
And he said, if you look at the life
of Jesus, he tells us to do three things.

01:10:31.355 --> 01:10:34.082
Preach, teach, and heal.
Church doesn't get a pass on the third one.

01:10:34.149 --> 01:10:35.032
Oh.

01:10:35.099 --> 01:10:39.063
And so his big thing is that
this place is supported by churches

01:10:39.130 --> 01:10:42.418
that actually don't take any federal money.

01:10:42.485 --> 01:10:46.598
They'll take the state,
Medicaid things for children, et cetera,

01:10:46.665 --> 01:10:49.507
but they support it all
through fundraising.

01:10:49.574 --> 01:10:54.096
There's a teaching kitchen here where
they work with doctors and their patients

01:10:54.163 --> 01:10:55.880
on eating and cooking healthily

01:10:55.947 --> 01:10:58.369
because we know wellness
is not just about a prescription.

01:10:58.436 --> 01:10:59.932
-Oh, yeah. That's important.

01:10:59.999 --> 01:11:01.981
-You come here to
get your prescription,

01:11:02.048 --> 01:11:04.848
but then you look up
and there's wellness being produced.

01:11:04.915 --> 01:11:07.593
So there's a 25,000-square-foot
YMCA up there.

01:11:07.660 --> 01:11:09.556
Right above us is a dental clinic.

01:11:09.623 --> 01:11:13.396
What they found is one of the primary ways
somebody can increase their wage

01:11:13.463 --> 01:11:14.630
is fixing their teeth.

01:11:14.697 --> 01:11:17.415
And so they have a 32-chair dental clinic.

01:11:17.482 --> 01:11:21.188
It's the largest nonprofit dental clinic
like it in the country.

01:11:21.765 --> 01:11:23.583
-Great to meet you.
-Todd. Good work.

01:11:23.650 --> 01:11:26.212
-All right, my friend.
-Todd, take care, man.

01:11:26.279 --> 01:11:27.676
-Enjoy.
-I'm loving it.

01:11:27.743 --> 01:11:30.508
He's about to find out
what barbecue bologna is.

01:11:33.193 --> 01:11:36.620
[Peter] All right. This is the place.
Cozy Corner BBQ.

01:11:38.040 --> 01:11:38.923
Now hiring.

01:11:40.684 --> 01:11:43.105
-[woman 1] Enjoy.
-[woman 2] Okay. I will.

01:11:43.770 --> 01:11:46.003
That dude right there is the founder.

01:11:46.070 --> 01:11:47.426
Where are all the pictures?

01:11:47.493 --> 01:11:48.799
All the pictures are gone.

01:11:48.866 --> 01:11:52.394
But everybody that knows anything
about barbecue has eaten here.

01:11:52.461 --> 01:11:55.983
Dignitaries, notables,
and it's been right here the whole time.

01:11:56.050 --> 01:11:59.866
That guy's wife, who is now,
I think, in her 90s,

01:11:59.933 --> 01:12:01.606
went to Manassas High School,

01:12:01.673 --> 01:12:04.040
and they opened this restaurant
a long time ago,

01:12:04.107 --> 01:12:05.578
the family still rubs it.

01:12:06.260 --> 01:12:08.021
Okay. Walk us through this, Bill.

01:12:08.088 --> 01:12:09.323
Barbecue Cornish hen.

01:12:09.813 --> 01:12:10.893
Barbecue Bologna.

01:12:10.960 --> 01:12:11.843
Ribs.

01:12:13.000 --> 01:12:15.731
-Barbecue spaghetti.
-Never seen or heard of that.

01:12:15.798 --> 01:12:17.375
It's because it's killer.

01:12:17.442 --> 01:12:19.040
And pulled pork sandwich.

01:12:19.438 --> 01:12:20.782
Barbecue spaghetti.

01:12:20.849 --> 01:12:25.062
-It's got a little barbecue meat in it.
-Instead of pasta sauce, it's barbecue?

01:12:25.129 --> 01:12:27.324
It's got pasta sauce and barbecue in it.

01:12:27.825 --> 01:12:29.879
Little Italian influence here, Bill?

01:12:29.946 --> 01:12:32.620
Little Italian barbecue influence.

01:12:35.419 --> 01:12:36.364
Huh.

01:12:36.431 --> 01:12:37.726
-Pretty good, isn't it?
-Yeah.

01:12:37.793 --> 01:12:38.683
Yeah.

01:12:39.280 --> 01:12:40.483
Barbecue Bologna.

01:12:40.550 --> 01:12:42.043
-Okay.
-And it has slaw on it.

01:12:42.110 --> 01:12:43.337
Just suck it up.

01:12:43.404 --> 01:12:44.672
Just go for it.

01:12:44.739 --> 01:12:47.974
And all you do is you pick it up
like a normal sandwich,

01:12:49.508 --> 01:12:50.424
shove it in.

01:12:53.594 --> 01:12:54.535
Stupid isn't it?

01:12:56.511 --> 01:12:59.158
-I've never tasted that before.
-[Bill laughs]

01:13:00.453 --> 01:13:01.336
All right.

01:13:01.813 --> 01:13:04.269
Cornish hen, I'm gonna peel off this side.

01:13:04.336 --> 01:13:06.397
It's smoked and then barbecued.

01:13:07.093 --> 01:13:09.376
And I don't know how they do it.

01:13:09.996 --> 01:13:12.723
But they keep it tender as cr*p.

01:13:12.790 --> 01:13:15.654
So you just get in there
with the fingers, Indian style.

01:13:15.721 --> 01:13:19.133
Just get in there. I'm eating
off this half. You eat off that half.

01:13:19.200 --> 01:13:23.506
Just get in there and get you
a big old mess out like that and pop it.

01:13:23.573 --> 01:13:24.456
Just pop?

01:13:25.662 --> 01:13:27.435
I see why you're doing the fingers.

01:13:27.502 --> 01:13:30.226
Just get it with your fingers, dude.
You're in Memphis.

01:13:30.293 --> 01:13:32.415
You can't eat nice with this sh*t.

01:13:32.482 --> 01:13:34.219
I got to put it right up here.

01:13:34.286 --> 01:13:37.899
Bring someone from out of town
trying to eat  barbecue with a fork.

01:13:37.966 --> 01:13:40.841
So that's what you want, right?
Just crisp skin.

01:13:40.908 --> 01:13:45.732
Just all of it. Skin, everything in there.
All the flavors coming together for you.

01:13:48.454 --> 01:13:49.681
-That's good.
-Yeah.

01:13:50.491 --> 01:13:52.291
-I like that.
-And then a rib.

01:13:52.792 --> 01:13:55.431
And they do their ribs
different than a lot.

01:13:55.498 --> 01:13:58.224
They slow cook them
and do something with the barbecue.

01:13:58.291 --> 01:14:00.622
-And get that charred on the outside.
-Okay.

01:14:01.097 --> 01:14:03.287
But I'm a huge fan of this place's ribs.

01:14:03.354 --> 01:14:06.754
Now, there are really good ribs
in Memphis and a lot of other places.

01:14:06.821 --> 01:14:08.988
But these are unique
and a little different.

01:14:09.055 --> 01:14:10.114
Been here forever.

01:14:13.240 --> 01:14:14.299
God, it's so good.

01:14:15.279 --> 01:14:16.808
You're gonna love the rib.

01:14:21.035 --> 01:14:23.604
Smoky, crispy on the outside,
tender on the inside,

01:14:23.671 --> 01:14:25.410
and then the sauce is killer.

01:14:30.303 --> 01:14:31.303
How good is that?

01:14:32.903 --> 01:14:34.356
-Wow.
-I know.

01:14:34.423 --> 01:14:35.527
How do they cook that?

01:14:35.594 --> 01:14:38.173
That's a really, really Memphis-y taste.

01:14:38.676 --> 01:14:39.559
The smoke.

01:14:40.478 --> 01:14:44.423
Tender, crispy on the outside,
and the sweet sauce all flavored together.

01:14:44.490 --> 01:14:47.261
-Oh, yeah.
-That's a very typical Memphis rib taste.

01:14:47.328 --> 01:14:49.126
Okay. How do they cook this?

01:14:49.193 --> 01:14:52.115
-Just simmering forever?
-In the old smoke room back there.

01:14:52.182 --> 01:14:54.024
They smoke stuff all night long.

01:14:54.482 --> 01:14:57.685
And then the last one
is your pulled pork sandwich.

01:14:58.330 --> 01:15:02.036
Why don't you bite that
and then tell me what you like the most.

01:15:02.485 --> 01:15:03.368
I'm curious.

01:15:03.933 --> 01:15:08.378
They just drip it in the barbecue.
They go abundance with the barbecue.

01:15:08.445 --> 01:15:09.328
Yeah.

01:15:14.923 --> 01:15:15.806
There we go.

01:15:16.305 --> 01:15:17.834
-That pretty good?
-Mm-hmm.

01:15:18.736 --> 01:15:19.619
That's good.

01:15:22.344 --> 01:15:23.260
Mmm.

01:15:27.310 --> 01:15:29.148
The sauce has some kick, you know?

01:15:29.215 --> 01:15:31.967
Well, you've got the hot.
These are all normal.

01:15:32.034 --> 01:15:34.139
But it's got a good kick. It's cool.

01:15:34.206 --> 01:15:35.508
What do you like the most?

01:15:35.575 --> 01:15:38.763
I'm not really a rib guy traditionally,
but I'd say the ribs.

01:15:38.830 --> 01:15:40.145
-Really?
-And this.

01:15:40.212 --> 01:15:42.379
That's a compliment
if you're not a rib guy.

01:15:42.446 --> 01:15:43.439
Not really.

01:15:43.506 --> 01:15:44.977
But I love a good sand-o.

01:15:45.440 --> 01:15:46.613
And this is hitting.

01:15:46.680 --> 01:15:49.852
My fingers are committed
and indented into this bun right now,

01:15:49.919 --> 01:15:51.272
so I'm gonna stay here.

01:15:52.262 --> 01:15:53.145
You're in now.

01:15:57.916 --> 01:16:00.403
Bill, that was awesome.
Thank you. Appreciate it.

01:16:00.470 --> 01:16:02.616
Listen, an honor to show off my town.

01:16:02.683 --> 01:16:04.271
No, I can feel it. Love it.

01:16:04.833 --> 01:16:09.395
And I want you guys to know Bill has
an awesome documentary, Undefeated.

01:16:09.462 --> 01:16:11.281
I'm gonna leave the link down below.

01:16:11.348 --> 01:16:13.683
-And also a podcast.
-Yeah.

01:16:13.750 --> 01:16:15.513
Where you're getting a human story.

01:16:15.580 --> 01:16:17.491
It's called An Army of Normal Folks.

01:16:17.558 --> 01:16:19.518
It's based around this idea.

01:16:20.070 --> 01:16:25.003
Government's proven woefully inadequate
in serving the least advantaged among us.

01:16:25.070 --> 01:16:28.736
There's neighborhoods all over the place
that we drive by that we see

01:16:28.803 --> 01:16:30.424
abject despair and loss,

01:16:30.956 --> 01:16:33.456
and we don't want to have
our car break down there.

01:16:33.523 --> 01:16:37.703
But as we cruise by and we look back down
that street or over that viaduct,

01:16:37.770 --> 01:16:39.484
we think to ourselves, man,

01:16:39.551 --> 01:16:41.871
somebody outta do something
about that one day.

01:16:41.938 --> 01:16:44.975
-Honestly, I want to know who's somebody.
-Okay.

01:16:45.042 --> 01:16:48.261
Maybe we need to kick
that rearview mirror 15 degrees to the left

01:16:48.328 --> 01:16:51.023
and say maybe I could do something
about that one day.

01:16:51.090 --> 01:16:55.122
And I don't think
the smart people on Fox and CNN,

01:16:55.189 --> 01:16:58.013
and I don't think
the smart people in DC and LA

01:16:58.596 --> 01:16:59.715
have fixed anything.

01:16:59.782 --> 01:17:03.584
If anything, I think they're probably
incented by an enormous amount of power

01:17:03.651 --> 01:17:06.458
to continue to craft
narratives that divide us.

01:17:06.525 --> 01:17:08.002
They're not helping anything.

01:17:08.069 --> 01:17:11.318
And I think the anecdote
to all of what's ailing us

01:17:11.385 --> 01:17:12.843
is an army of normal folks.

01:17:12.910 --> 01:17:16.206
Guys like you and me saying,
hey, here's what I'm good at.

01:17:16.646 --> 01:17:20.257
Here's what I'm passionate about.
Here's an area of need in my community.

01:17:20.324 --> 01:17:23.083
Why don't I just engage
and try to help one person?

01:17:23.150 --> 01:17:26.281
And if we had millions of people
across our country doing that,

01:17:26.348 --> 01:17:30.097
literally an army of normal folks
engaging where they can, how they can,

01:17:30.164 --> 01:17:32.314
I think we could change a lot what ails us.

01:17:32.381 --> 01:17:36.146
And so the podcast tells stories
of people doing that very thing.

01:17:37.149 --> 01:17:39.222
-Good stuff, Bill.
-Yeah, this was fun.

01:17:39.289 --> 01:17:41.870
Guys, thanks for coming along
on that journey.

01:17:42.351 --> 01:17:43.469
Until the next one.

01:17:43.536 --> 01:17:45.815
[jazz music]

